tIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"German plans were adjusted after Italy signalled it would not immediately enter the war; the claimed six-day causation is historically disputed.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":691,"stem":"What were the two principal intersecting streets in the ideal Roman town plan called?","choices":["Cardine and decumanico","Forum and agora","Centuria and decuria","Cardo and decumanus","None of these"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The cardo ran roughly north–south and the decumanus east–west.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":692,"stem":"For what is Hippodamus of Miletus famous?","choices":["Urban-planning ideas","Historical writings","Sculpture","Military strategy","Inventing the Ionic order"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Hippodamus is associated with rational grid plans for Greek cities.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":693,"stem":"Which pair includes two contemporaries of Gian Lorenzo Bernini?","choices":["Raphael and Filippino Lippi","Borromini and Baldassare Peruzzi","Pietro da Cortona and Borromini","Michelangelo and Pietro da Cortona","Perugino and Benedetto Antelami"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Bernini, Borromini and Pietro da Cortona all worked in seventeenth-century Rome.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":694,"stem":"When did the Eastern Roman Empire end?","choices":["741 CE","1453 CE","1048 CE","1215 CE","476 CE"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Constantinople fell to the Ottomans in 1453.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":695,"stem":"Which building do these elevations depict?","choices":["Casa Batlló, Antoni Gaudí","Rufer House, Adolf Loos","Villa Savoye, Le Corbusier","Fallingwater, Frank Lloyd Wright","Barcelona Pavilion, Mies van der Rohe"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The compact elevations and shifted openings correspond to Loos's Rufer House.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B008-Q695","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3499.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[174,941,515,1018]],"method":"source_figure_crop","essential":true},"sourceImage":"IMG_3499.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"17710ea795b613374bb13d5ac5ff9b8bc59a5eb784effec267ced2cd5c707d27"},{"qid":696,"stem":"Which list places these popes in chronological order?","choices":["Linus; John XXIII; Pius XII","Peter; Celestine V; Linus","Linus; Boniface VIII; Pius XII","Gregory the Great; Benedict XVI; Alexander Farnese","John XXIII; Pius XII; Linus"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Linus succeeded Peter in the first century, Boniface VIII lived in the thirteenth century, Pius XII in the twentieth.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":697,"stem":"Why was the eighteenth century called the Age of Enlightenment?","choices":["Building techniques enlarged openings","Electricity was invented","Paintings became brighter","Reason-centred philosophical thought developed","Religious influence dominated every field"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Enlightenment thinkers emphasised rational inquiry and criticism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":698,"stem":"Which style is Borromini's San Carlo alle Quattro Fontane façade?","choices":["Renaissance","Gothic","Baroque","Rococo","Mannerist"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The curved, dynamic façade is characteristic of Roman Baroque architecture.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":699,"stem":"Match each fresco cycle to its site.\n1. Mantegna's Camera degli Sposi; 2. Raphael's School of Athens; 3. Michelangelo's Flood; 4. Leonardo's Last Supper; 5. Giotto's Last Judgment\na. Milan, Santa Maria delle Grazie refectory; b. Vatican, Sistine Chapel vault; c. Mantua, Palazzo Ducale; d. Padua, Scrovegni Chapel; e. Vatican, Stanza della Segnatura","choices":["1–c; 2–a; 3–e; 4–b; 5–d","1–a; 2–c; 3–b; 4–d; 5–e","1–c; 2–e; 3–b; 4–a; 5–d","1–a; 2–e; 3–d; 4–b; 5–c","1–b; 2–c; 3–d; 4–a; 5–e"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The sites are respectively Mantua, the Vatican Stanza, the Sistine vault, Milan and Padua.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":700,"stem":"Which of these buildings is NOT in Mantua?","choices":["Clock Tower in Piazza delle Erbe","Palazzo Te","Basilica of Sant'Andrea","Palazzo Ducale","Palazzo dei Diamanti"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Palazzo dei Diamanti is in Ferrara.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":701,"stem":"Arrange these buildings by date.\na. Basilica of Superga; b. Ca' Brutta; c. Mausoleum of Galla Placidia; d. Basilica Palladiana, Vicenza; e. Doge's Palace, Venice","choices":["c; d; e; b; a","d; c; e; b; a","c; e; d; b; a","c; d; e; a; b","c; e; d; a; b"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Ravenna mausoleum precedes medieval Venice, Palladio's Renaissance basilica, the eighteenth-century Superga and twentieth-century Ca' Brutta.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":702,"stem":"Who painted the pictured work?","choices":["Édouard Manet","Edgar Degas","Jacques-Louis David","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","René Magritte"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The composition is associated with Ingres's classicising painting.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":703,"stem":"Which event did NOT occur in the twentieth century?","choices":["Discovery of penicillin","Deportations to Soviet gulags","Creation of a global drug market","Food supply chains","Discovery of the steam engine"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Steam engines were developed before the twentieth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":704,"stem":"Which architectural element is NOT characteristic of Gothic architecture?","choices":["Pointed arch","Clustered piers","Buttresses","Rose window","Small splayed windows"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Gothic churches typically seek larger window openings than Romanesque buildings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":705,"stem":"Which twentieth-century artist revisited Velázquez's Las Meninas?","choices":["Pablo Picasso","Edward Hopper","Marc Chagall","Giorgio Morandi","Giorgio de Chirico"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Picasso made a 1957 series interpreting Las Meninas.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":706,"stem":"Which is NOT characteristic of Futurism?","choices":["Movement and simultaneity","Strong patriotism","Displaying everyday found objects as artworks","Glorification of machines and speed","Breaking with the past"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Found objects used as artworks are linked especially to Dada and Duchamp.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":707,"stem":"How was Julius Caesar related by birth to his adopted heir Octavian?","choices":["His uncle","His grandfather","His cousin","His father-in-law","His biological father"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Caesar was the great-uncle of Octavian; the source simplifies this to “uncle.”","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":708,"stem":"Who sculpted the bronze shown?","choices":["Filippo Tommaso Marinetti","Umberto Boccioni","Fortunato Depero","Giacomo Balla","Carlo Carrà"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Boccioni's Unique Forms of Continuity in Space conveys Futurist movement.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B008-Q708","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3500.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[814,1117,957,1311]],"method":"source_figure_crop","essential":true},"sourceImage":"IMG_3500.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"19d25178cea85d1fa627f1bc073fdf8f0bc5e4b37f53458c7e890ff81a41e97f"},{"qid":709,"stem":"Which style is the pictured building?","choices":["High-tech","Deconstructivism","Rationalism","Neoplasticism","Postmodernism"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Rietveld Schröder House exemplifies De Stijl and Neoplasticism.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":710,"stem":"Which element is NOT characteristic of Romanesque architecture?","choices":["Pointed arch","Round arch","Groin vault","Rose window","Two-colour bands"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The pointed arch is associated especially with Gothic architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":711,"stem":"Which order is chronological?","choices":["Romanesque; Baroque; Gothic; Renaissance; Mannerism","Romanesque; Gothic; Renaissance; Mannerism; Baroque","Baroque; Romanesque; Gothic; Mannerism; Renaissance","Gothic; Romanesque; Baroque; Renaissance; Mannerism","Romanesque; Mannerism; Baroque; Renaissance; Gothic"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The sequence follows medieval, Renaissance, Mannerist and Baroque periods.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":712,"stem":"Which adjective best describes Baroque style?","choices":["Sober","Formal","Symbolic","Elaborate","Rigid"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Baroque art often uses dramatic, elaborate effects.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":713,"stem":"Which event–date pairing is wrong?","choices":["1717 – opening of the Council of Trent","387 – Augustine's conversion","1804 – Napoleon crowned emperor","1453 – fall of Constantinople","31 BCE – Battle of Actium"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Council of Trent opened in 1545; the other date associations are accepted in the source's broad chronology.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":714,"stem":"What does apartheid mean?","choices":["A policy of racial segregation","Political secession","Religious secession","Political affiliation","Religious affiliation"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Apartheid was a system of institutionalised racial segregation.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":715,"stem":"Which country owns Antarctica?","choices":["Argentina","Chile","Brazil","Australia","No single nation"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"No country has sovereignty over Antarctica as a whole under the Antarctic Treaty framework.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":716,"stem":"What element has the chemical symbol Na?","choices":["Potassium","Sodium","Nitrogen","Copper","Niobium"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Na derives from the Latin name natrium for sodium.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":717,"stem":"Which country is NOT a member of the European Union?","choices":["Lithuania","Türkiye","France","Romania","Sweden"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Türkiye is not an EU member.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":718,"stem":"Which work was NOT written by William Shakespeare?","choices":["The Taming of the Shrew","A Midsummer Night's Dream","The Tempest","The Merry Wives of Windsor","Lady Windermere's Fan"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Lady Windermere's Fan is by Oscar Wilde.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":719,"id":"ALPHA-B008-Q719","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Contemporary","label":"Previdenza in Italian","stem":"Which Italian word can refer to social security or provision for future needs, rather than specifically supernatural clairvoyance?","choices":["Divinazione","Intuizione","Previdenza","Preveggenza","Intuito"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"Previdenza refers to prudent provision for the future and, in expressions such as previdenza sociale, social security. The original vague synonym exclusion is clarified.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3501.jpeg","examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"8ab6defba55f47801083ad324aa4c2e68fd80548059ed02e5043a4b10f6b4889"},{"qid":720,"id":"ALPHA-B008-Q720","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Contemporary","label":"Italian subject-verb agreement","stem":"Which Italian sentence contains a subject–verb agreement error?","choices":["La ragazza con la quale parlavo è Rita","Guardati da colui che sparla di tutti","Il territorio in cui ci troviamo è brullo","A chiunque telefoni di' che io siamo occupato","Il forte acquazzone ci ha colti alla sprovvista"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"Io requires sono, not siamo. The source's original fourth sentence can be grammatical, so its defect is made explicit.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3501.jpeg","examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"ecc8e4c434046a5691671b3826a763ba875495e8a8d43c6f16924355d6ab13a3"},{"qid":721,"stem":"Which twentieth-century avant-garde artist gave his name to a particular shade of blue?","choices":["Jean Tinguely","Yves Klein","Piero Manzoni","Mark Rothko","Barnett Newman"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"International Klein Blue is associated with Yves Klein.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":722,"stem":"What does iconology study?","choices":["Only the subject depicted","The correct chronology of an artwork","The meaning of images, symbols and allegories","The technique used to execute an artwork","The attribution of an artwork"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Iconology interprets the meanings of images and symbols.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":723,"stem":"Who designed the Unités d'Habitation?","choices":["Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","Alvar Aalto","Charles-Édouard Jeanneret","Walter Gropius","Gio Ponti"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Charles-Édouard Jeanneret, known as Le Corbusier, designed them.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":724,"stem":"Why did Adolf Loos reject ornament in architecture?","choices":["He unconditionally supported Art Nouveau","He sought beauty in volumes and structure","He anticipated 1960s minimalism","He favored technical drawing","He opposed contemporary Baroque"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Loos argued that form and structure could carry architecture without applied ornament.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":725,"stem":"Which artist painted the work shown?","choices":["Wassily Kandinsky","Carlo Carrà","Jackson Pollock","Joan Miró","Paul Klee"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The abstract composition is by Wassily Kandinsky.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":726,"stem":"Which Gaudí building is shown in the photograph?","choices":["Sagrada Família","Parc Güell","Casa Batlló","Palau Güell","La Pedrera"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The sculptural rooftop chimneys identify Casa Milà, also called La Pedrera.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":727,"stem":"What kind of object is Bruno Munari's Cubo?","choices":["An armchair","An ashtray","A toy","A television set","A lamp"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Munari's Cubo is an ashtray.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31","imageCredit93":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/1193","imageNote93":"Museum object photograph added for study; not an Alpha page reproduction."},{"qid":728,"stem":"Piero della Francesca was a:","choices":["Fifteenth-century artist","Pupil of Leonardo da Vinci","Sixteenth-century Tuscan architect","Sixteenth-century sculptor","Lombard Gothic painter"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Piero della Francesca was a fifteenth-century painter.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":729,"stem":"Who designed the Juicy Salif citrus squeezer shown?","choices":["Enzo Mari","Marco Zanuso","Ron Arad","Andrea Branzi","None of these"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Juicy Salif was designed by Philippe Starck.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":730,"stem":"What artistic style characterized Europe roughly between 1400 and 1540?","choices":["Romanesque","Mannerism","Gothic","Baroque","Renaissance"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Renaissance spans much of this period in European art.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":731,"stem":"Which part of the church of Saint-Rémi in Reims is shown?","choices":["A side aisle","The apse","The ambulatory","The transept","The central nave"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The photograph looks down the church's central nave.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":732,"stem":"Monet said that everything changes, even stone, while studying the west façade of which cathedral?","choices":["Reims Cathedral","Cologne Cathedral","Rouen Cathedral","Amiens Cathedral","Notre-Dame de Paris"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Monet made a series of paintings of Rouen Cathedral's façade.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":733,"stem":"What is a typical plan for an ancient Greek temple?","choices":["A rectangular naos preceded by a columned pronaos","A rectangle without colonnades","A square with a porch","A trapezoid with an entrance on its broad side","A square that is always peripteral"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The main sanctuary, or naos, typically follows the entrance porch, or pronaos.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":734,"stem":"The title of Arman's sculpture shown refers to which painter?","choices":["César","Picasso","Munch","Matisse","Cézanne"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Arman's sculpture is called Picassienne, a reference to Picasso.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":735,"stem":"From which ancient theatre did Western European theatre inherit its basic structural concept?","choices":["The Roman theatre","The Greek theatre","The Roman domus","The Greek temple","The Roman basilica"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The textbook identifies the Roman theatre as the structural precedent.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":736,"stem":"Which event did NOT occur in 1936?","choices":["Hitler came to power","The Spanish Civil War broke out","The French Popular Front won the election","A dictatorship was established in Greece","None of these"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Hitler came to power in 1933, three years earlier.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":737,"stem":"What material enabled Roman builders to construct large vaults?","choices":["Ashlar blocks","Brick alone","Opus caementicium","Wood","Straw"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Opus caementicium is Roman concrete, suitable for broad vaults.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":738,"stem":"Which ancient theatre typically used a natural slope for its seating?","choices":["Etruscan","Celtic","Greek","Roman","None of these"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Greek theatres commonly set their seating into a hillside.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":739,"stem":"Which pairing of an Italian public figure and profession is incorrect?","choices":["Giovanni Falcone — magistrate","Aldo Moro — politician","Piersanti Mattarella — politician","Luigi Calabresi — police commissioner","Carlo Alberto dalla Chiesa — magistrate"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Dalla Chiesa was a Carabinieri general, not a magistrate.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":740,"stem":"What is Bakelite?","choices":["Inflammation of the knee joint","A lidded crystal box","A reinforcement for a bridge pier","A slaughterhouse","An artificial material"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Bakelite is an early synthetic plastic.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":741,"id":"ALPHA-B008-Q741","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Modern","label":"Claudio Magris's generation","stem":"Which Italian writer was born after the First World War?","choices":["Claudio Magris","Elsa Morante","Alberto Moravia","Filippo Tommaso Marinetti","Eugenio Montale"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"Magris was born in 1939; the other writers were born before 1914. This supplies a clear criterion for the source's unspecified odd-one-out.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3504.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"8336414dee18505e57116952ec70ca5fcb8ac3c67b64ae7299befa3959d5ae61"},{"qid":742,"stem":"What does the Italian expression acqua e sapone describe in a person?","choices":["Dissolute","Natural and genuine","Naive","Mean","Miserly"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"It describes an unpretentious, naturally genuine person.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":743,"stem":"Which dance and country pairing is incorrect?","choices":["Bharatanatyam — India","Samba — Brazil","Mambo — Cuba","Haka — New Zealand","Flamenco — Argentina"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Flamenco is associated with Spain, especially Andalusia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":744,"stem":"Which sequence gives the cities of Palazzo Grassi, Palazzo Rosso, Palazzo Farnese, Palazzo Pandolfini and Palazzo Chiericati, in that order?","choices":["Rome; Vicenza; Florence; Genoa; Venice","Venice; Genoa; Rome; Florence; Vicenza","Genoa; Rome; Vicenza; Venice; Florence","Venice; Vicenza; Rome; Florence; Genoa","Rome; Genoa; Vicenza; Venice; Florence"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The palaces are respectively in Venice, Genoa, Rome, Florence and Vicenza.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":745,"stem":"Which architect and painter belong to the same artistic movement?","choices":["Renzo Piano and Canaletto","Antonio Sant'Elia and Umberto Boccioni","Leon Battista Alberti and Caravaggio","Gian Lorenzo Bernini and Giorgione","Andrea Palladio and Simone Martini"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Sant'Elia and Boccioni were both associated with Futurism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":746,"stem":"Which New York museum is shown?","choices":["Museum of Modern Art","Metropolitan Museum of Art","Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum","Fogg Museum","Seagram Building"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The spiral form is Frank Lloyd Wright's Guggenheim Museum.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":747,"id":"ALPHA-B008-Q747","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Contemporary","label":"Italian present participle curante","stem":"Which Italian word is the present participle of the verb curare?","choices":["Aborigeno","Curante","Devolution","Notazione","Tumulto"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"Curante is the present participle of curare. A specific grammatical criterion replaces the source's unspecified exclusion rule.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3504.jpeg","examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"aa5470d57ecedeacb2388469db1ba00fd99ebc7654388ad5e324e0c7788a59fd"},{"qid":748,"stem":"What was Leo Tolstoy's nationality?","choices":["Swiss","Polish","Hungarian","Russian","Czech"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Tolstoy was a Russian writer.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":749,"stem":"What event is commemorated by Italian streets named XX Settembre?","choices":["An Allied armistice","The victory at Vittorio Veneto","The capture of Rome at Porta Pia","The first Italian Parliament","The declaration of the Italian Republic"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Italian troops entered Rome through Porta Pia on 20 September 1870.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":750,"id":"ALPHA-B008-Q750","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Vitruvius and Renaissance treatises","stem":"Which architect wrote his architectural treatise in antiquity, rather than belonging to the fifteenth-century Renaissance?","choices":["Leon Battista Alberti","Antonio Averlino, known as Filarete","Marcus Vitruvius Pollio","Francesco di Giorgio Martini","Donato Bramante"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"Vitruvius wrote De architectura in the first century BC. The original exclusion based on fifteenth-century treatise authorship also raises questions about Bramante, so the period is specified.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3505.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"38d19c572dc59a47200acf998228cac0677730b8e01a5637445849035a8f187b"},{"qid":751,"stem":"When was the American Civil War fought?","choices":["In the 1840s","In the late eighteenth century","In the 1810s","In the second half of the nineteenth century","In the last decade of the nineteenth century"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The war lasted from 1861 to 1865.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":752,"stem":"Which country experienced the dictatorship of the colonels?","choices":["Spain","Greece","Portugal","Chile","Libya"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Greece's military junta ruled from 1967 to 1974.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":753,"stem":"What did the English Navigation Act of 1651 seek to require?","choices":["Unrestricted trade with every colony","Trade with England and its colonies on English vessels","Dutch trade with English colonies","English trade exclusively with Dutch merchants","Only Dutch vessels between England and its colonies"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The law restricted transport in English trade to English ships or qualifying ships of origin.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":754,"stem":"What happened in Italy on 25 July 1943?","choices":["The armistice was signed","The Salò Republic was established","Mussolini was arrested","The Casablanca Conference opened","The Fosse Ardeatine massacre occurred"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Mussolini was removed from power and arrested on that date.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":755,"stem":"Who opposed each other at the Battle of Actium in 31 BCE?","choices":["Caesar and Pompey","Octavian and Pompey","Caesar and Octavian","Mark Antony and Caesar","Octavian and Mark Antony"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Octavian's fleet defeated the forces of Antony and Cleopatra.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":756,"stem":"What did the art-historical term Mannerism originally emphasize?","choices":["Counter-Reformation art","Artists following the manner of great predecessors","Art's complete loss of originality","The morning of a new movement","Only spiritual tension"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The term refers to artistic maniera and the style of celebrated predecessors.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":757,"stem":"In which year did the Congress of Vienna conclude?","choices":["1814","1816","1817","1815","1812"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Congress concluded in 1815.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":758,"stem":"In which conflict did a submarine first carry out a successful attack on an enemy warship?","choices":["The American Civil War","The Russian Revolution","The Third Italian War of Independence","The War of the Fifth Coalition","The Franco-Prussian War"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Confederate submarine H. L. Hunley sank a warship in 1864.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":759,"stem":"How many Pompeian painting styles are conventionally distinguished?","choices":["Three","Two","Five","Four","Six"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The standard classification recognizes four styles.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":760,"stem":"Which two countries fought the Falklands War in 1982?","choices":["Argentina and Chile","Ecuador and Colombia","Ecuador and Chile","Argentina and Uruguay","The United Kingdom and Argentina"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The war was between the United Kingdom and Argentina.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":761,"stem":"In which year did Antoni Gaudí die?","choices":["1916","1956","1966","1976","1926"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The architect Antoni Gaudí died in 1926.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":762,"stem":"What happened to Alexander the Great's empire after his death?","choices":["It was divided into four major parts","His treasurer Harpalus ruled it","Egypt absorbed it","The Persian Empire absorbed it","It immediately dissolved into many tiny states"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The successor kingdoms are commonly summarized as four principal divisions.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":763,"stem":"Which event occurred after the conventional beginning of the Modern Age?","choices":["The fall of Constantinople","The fall of Granada","Columbus's arrival in America","Cromwell's revolution","Marco Polo's journey to China"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The English Revolution occurred in the seventeenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":764,"stem":"What was Gaudenzio Ferrari's profession?","choices":["Painter","Architect","Playwright","Poet","Engineer"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Gaudenzio Ferrari was an Italian Renaissance painter.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":765,"stem":"In which century did the Protestant Reformation begin?","choices":["Eighteenth","Sixteenth","Fifteenth","Seventeenth","Fourteenth"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Luther's 1517 challenge began in the sixteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":766,"stem":"What is bucchero?","choices":["A painting technique","A civic building","A temple","A genre of poetry","A kind of vase"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Bucchero is black Etruscan pottery, including vases.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":767,"stem":"What was Luddism?","choices":["The enclosure of common pastures","The transatlantic slave trade","Opposition to machines in industry","The exclusion of Catholics from politics","An army spreading plague"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Luddites protested mechanization in British industry.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":768,"stem":"Who made the sculpture shown?","choices":["Alberto Giacometti","Marino Marini","Arturo Martini","Francesco Messina","Giacomo Manzù"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The elongated walking figure is by Alberto Giacometti.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":769,"stem":"Which doctrine opposed European intervention in the Americas and expressed American noninvolvement in European affairs?","choices":["The Monroe Doctrine","The New Deal","The Declaratory Act","The Bill of Rights","The Tenth Amendment"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Monroe Doctrine addressed European colonization and intervention in the Americas.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":770,"stem":"Which country invaded Afghanistan in 1979 and replaced Hafizullah Amin with Babrak Karmal?","choices":["NATO","Iran","Iraq","The United States","The Soviet Union"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Soviet Union intervened and installed Karmal.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":771,"stem":"Which of these artists was a Surrealist?","choices":["Mimmo Paladino","Keith Haring","Salvador Dalí","Fernando Botero","Lucio Fontana"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Salvador Dalí was a leading Surrealist artist.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":772,"stem":"Which date is a national holiday in the United States?","choices":["20 September","25 April","4 July","12 October","3 May"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Independence Day is celebrated on 4 July.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":773,"stem":"The figures show three columns belonging to different architectural orders. Which option correctly matches each image to its order?","choices":["A – Doric; B – Corinthian; C – Ionic","A – Doric; B – Ionic; C – Corinthian","A – Ionic; B – Tuscan; C – Minoan","A – Tuscan; B – Ionic; C – Minoan","A – Ionic; B – Minoan; C – Tuscan"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Doric has a simple capital, Ionic has scroll-like volutes, and Corinthian has acanthus leaves.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":774,"stem":"What is a lesena, or pilaster strip?","choices":["A small porch at the entrance to a Romanesque church, shaped like a small aedicule supported by columns","A gallery above the aisles of a church, usually reserved for women","A spherical triangular part of a vault below the drum","A decorative half-pier or half-column projecting slightly from a wall","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A lesena is a shallow vertical decorative projection on a wall.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":775,"stem":"The sign shown is an icon of which style?","choices":["Neoclassical","Rationalist","High-tech","Art Nouveau (Liberty)","Postmodern"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Paris Métro entrance's flowing organic ironwork is characteristic of Art Nouveau.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":776,"stem":"Which is regarded as the standard language for working with databases?","choices":["C++","SQL","Java","Access","Witold"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"SQL is used to query and manage relational databases.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":777,"stem":"Which language does NOT belong to the Romance language family?","choices":["Spanish","Catalan","French","Bulgarian","Romanian"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Bulgarian is a Slavic language; the other four developed from Latin.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":778,"stem":"Which author did NOT win the Nobel Prize in Literature?","choices":["Charles Dickens","Pablo Neruda","Harold Pinter","Thomas Mann","Bob Dylan"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Dickens died before the Nobel Prizes were established; the other four received the literature prize.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":779,"stem":"Who directed Amarcord?","choices":["François Truffaut","Alfred Hitchcock","Francis Ford Coppola","Federico Fellini","Stanley Kubrick"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Amarcord is a film by Federico Fellini.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":780,"stem":"Which book was NOT written by Primo Levi?","choices":["If This Is a Man","Beyond Good and Evil","The Truce","If Not Now, When?","The Drowned and the Saved"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Beyond Good and Evil is by Friedrich Nietzsche.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":781,"stem":"What does “anorexia” mean?","choices":["Inability to eat","Inability to retain food that has been eaten","Loss or reduction of appetite","Thinness","The desire to resemble a role model, usually a famous person"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The word means loss or reduction of appetite; it is not simply another word for thinness.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":782,"stem":"The name of German mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss is famously associated with:","choices":["The sine function","The law of supply and demand","The normal distribution","The uncertainty principle","Bayes' theorem"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The normal distribution is also called the Gaussian distribution.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":783,"stem":"The objects shown clearly pay tribute to a famous pictorial composition by:","choices":["Joan Miró","Piet Mondrian","Roy Lichtenstein","Andy Warhol","Pablo Picasso"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Their black orthogonal grids and rectangular colour fields recall Mondrian's compositions.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":784,"stem":"Vanvitelli (a), Mengoni (b), Borromini (c), Filarete (d) and Palladio (e) were Italian architects of different periods. In what chronological order were they active?","choices":["d; e; c; a; b","e; c; b; a; d","c; a; e; d; b","d; a; b; c; e","b; e; d; c; a"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The sequence is Filarete, Palladio, Borromini, Vanvitelli and Mengoni.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":785,"stem":"Which painter worked in the eighteenth century?","choices":["Simone Martini","Luca Signorelli","Guido Reni","Giambattista Tiepolo","Giovanni Fattori"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Tiepolo is an eighteenth-century painter; the others belong to earlier or later periods.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":786,"stem":"What is the commonly used title of the Renoir painting shown?","choices":["Self-Portrait","Monet Reading","Sunflowers","The Enigma of a Day","Boy with a Pipe"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The portrait shows Claude Monet reading.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":787,"stem":"The painting shown is an early work by:","choices":["Vincent van Gogh","Joan Miró","Piet Mondrian","Roy Lichtenstein","Andy Warhol"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Mondrian painted increasingly abstract trees before developing his familiar grid compositions.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":788,"stem":"Put these works in chronological order: 1. Marilyn — Andy Warhol; 2. Dynamism of a Dog on a Leash — Giacomo Balla; 3. Casa del Fascio — Giuseppe Terragni; 4. Juicy Salif — Philippe Starck; 5. Chapel of Ronchamp — Le Corbusier.","choices":["2; 3; 5; 1; 4","4; 1; 5; 3; 2","2; 3; 1; 5; 4","2; 5; 1; 3; 4","2; 5; 3; 1; 4"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Balla's 1912 painting precedes Terragni's 1930s building, the 1950s Ronchamp chapel, Warhol's 1962 Marilyn and Starck's 1990 Juicy Salif. The duplicate correct sequence is replaced.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":788,"stem":"Put these works in chronological order: 1. Marilyn — Andy Warhol; 2. Dynamism of a Dog on a Leash — Giacomo Balla; 3. Casa del Fascio — Giuseppe Terragni; 4. Juicy Salif — Philippe Starck; 5. Chapel of Ronchamp — Le Corbusier.","choices":["2; 3; 5; 1; 4","4; 1; 5; 3; 2","2; 3; 1; 5; 4","2; 3; 5; 1; 4","2; 5; 3; 1; 4"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Balla's 1912 painting precedes Terragni's 1930s building, Le Corbusier's 1950s chapel, Warhol's 1962 Marilyn and Starck's 1990 juicer. Choices A and D are identical and both must be accepted.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B009-Q788","major":"Design","label":"Chronology: Futurism, modern architecture, Pop Art, industrial design","era":"Era not assigned","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3509.jpeg","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"63d95cdc317efcb224b4e9143a3a22a371f2f39e5cf428969856c1d006795545"},{"qid":789,"stem":"In a Roman theatre, the stage is bounded by:","choices":["A plain wall backdrop","A wall backdrop articulated as a fully developed architectural structure","An open wall backdrop framing the surrounding landscape","No boundary","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The scaenae frons is an elaborate architectural backdrop to the Roman stage.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":790,"stem":"What is the plan shape of the part of Pope Julius III's villa attributed to Vignola?","choices":["Square","An exedra inscribed within a rectangle","Pentagonal","Octagonal","Circular"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The source identifies an exedra set within a rectangular plan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":791,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q791","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Acroterion","stem":"What is an acroterion in a Greek temple?","choices":["A decorative feature at the apex and corners of the pediment","An articulation of an Ionic capital","A component of a Corinthian base","A male figure decorating a capital","None of the above"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Acroteria are ornaments placed at the peak and outer corners of a temple pediment.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3509.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"af603182ff58ed5c028a9340046b30039246f138b4b741164886a01a05d981b4"},{"qid":792,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q792","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"Women's suffrage in Portugal","stem":"Which of these European Union countries was the last to establish equal universal women's suffrage?","choices":["Portugal","Italy","Greece","Denmark","France"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Portugal established equal universal suffrage following the 1974 revolution, later enshrined in the 1976 Constitution.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3509.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"fdf18b1127d8f204a0717dad17987178ab0cc113ae5cc34f247206a8f224fa59"},{"qid":793,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q793","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"La Belle Ferronnière","stem":"Who painted La Belle Ferronnière?","choices":["Diego Velázquez","Leonardo da Vinci","El Greco","Peter Paul Rubens","Michelangelo"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"La Belle Ferronnière is attributed to Leonardo da Vinci and is held in the Louvre.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3509.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"aaaf044a1c7b07752e345f9bc476703e70537cdefef2625295648ec104e668c4"},{"qid":794,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q794","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","label":"Chronology of Italian buildings","stem":"Order these buildings chronologically: a. La Scala, Milan; b. Leaning Tower of Pisa; c. Olympic Stadium, Rome; d. Santa Maria della Salute, Venice; e. Mole Antonelliana, Turin.","choices":["a; b; c; e; d","b; d; a; e; c","d; e; c; b; a","b; a; d; e; c","a; b; d; e; c"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"