"explanation":"Its monumental vaulted space is commonly related to Bramante's contemporary plans for St Peter's.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3513.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"fa360fd14498829c7c9b0109cce61cd296bc0682e3b5fa4014dd3d0ccaddb373"},{"qid":834,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q834","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Ciborium","stem":"What does ciborium mean as an architectural element in a medieval church?","choices":["The part behind the altar reserved for singers","A canopy or aedicule over the altar","A triangular element crowning a façade","A room beneath the presbytery containing a martyr's relics","None of the above"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"An architectural ciborium is a canopy, commonly supported by columns, over the altar.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3513.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"d3c85da0a9fa06988264b7203c7acf12728e3f844800d135e52cf6d8ff7de202"},{"qid":835,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q835","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Greek and Roman architectural elements","stem":"Which statement is incorrect?","choices":["Greek architecture emphasizes balanced verticals, horizontals, solids and voids","Roman architecture makes substantial use of curved forms","The vault is characteristic of Roman architecture","The triglyph is the characteristic element of Roman architecture","The column is characteristic of Greek architecture"],"cor