ann's rebuilding of Paris took place under Napoleon III during the Second Empire.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"45b6dd0054b1098fb4d90ed0e1c148ce3edcbe7cb26311d619e72855c25dba13"},{"qid":840,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q840","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","label":"Italian architects in St Petersburg","stem":"Which nationality was especially prominent among the architects Peter the Great invited to develop St Petersburg?","choices":["English","Italian","Dutch","Russian","Spanish"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Architects from Italian-speaking regions, including Domenico Trezzini, played a major part in the city's early development.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"bc8f199f1c178c54520c1daba12070c1d43aecd55a34d69bc08e23cac86aab24"},{"qid":841,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q841","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Western Roman emperors","stem":"Which ruler was an Eastern Roman emperor rather than an emperor of the earlier unified Roman Empire?","choices":["Tiberius (14–37)","Vespasian (69–79)","Hadrian (117–138)","Caracalla (211–217)","Justinian I (527–565)"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Justinian ruled the Eastern Roman, or Byzantine, Empire in the sixth century. He was still a Roman emperor in that sense; the source's original exclusion is clarified here.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"E","contentFingerprint":"4db34406ae2942199f1e3f35295fde817ac70dfadc49e346f25bec9279a18304"},{"qid":842,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q842","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Gauguin's Tahitian figures","stem":"Who painted the work shown?","choices":["Paul Cézanne","Paul Gauguin","Vincent van Gogh","Claude Monet","Henri Matisse"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The flat colour areas and Tahitian figures identify Paul Gauguin's painting.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"aa758e24269e2e984ebfdc5ecbce9e5f739f918aaa76750230504b433728c0e1","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[249,1088,584,1340]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":335,"height":252},"visualSubject":"Gauguin's Tahitian figures","visualConcept":"Visual identification or ordering","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a verified public-domain/licensed reproduction or an original labelled schematic for generated variants."},{"qid":843,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q843","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"Italian political terrorism victims","stem":"Which pair were victims of left-wing terrorist organizations in Italy?","choices":["Paolo Borsellino and Aldo Moro","Giovanni Falcone and Aldo Moro","Aldo Moro and Walter Tobagi","Paolo Borsellino and Giovanni Falcone","Giovanni Falcone and Walter Tobagi"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Moro was killed by the Red Brigades; Tobagi by the Brigata XXVIII Marzo. Falcone and Borsellino were murdered by the Mafia. The source incorrectly attributes both Moro and Tobagi to the Red Brigades.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"ae096e06aabf26e4b4289c05985282d5f76b803feb21131c3ba9e0f2e215058d"},{"qid":844,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q844","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Historical","label":"Literary works and countries","stem":"Which literary work–country pairing is incorrect?","choices":["The Tale of Genji — Japan","Oedipus Rex — Greece","Rumi's poems — China","The Divine Comedy — Italy","Don Quixote — Spain"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Rumi wrote within the Persian literary tradition, not the Chinese tradition.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"b001cd6535bb2c1311439fe1a5ec436a9797c5b712b99ac389aa6191ff9c2924"},{"qid":845,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q845","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Modern","label":"Madama Butterfly","stem":"Who composed Madama Butterfly?","choices":["Giuseppe Verdi","Gioachino Rossini","Georges Bizet","Giacomo Puccini","Richard Wagner"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Puccini composed Madama Butterfly, first performed in 1904.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"6ce29df6e6dafb8bb4a7b18c8cb096c900178b9f36b418c1177c6a970794fcb5"},{"qid":846,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q846","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Modern","label":"The Flowers of Evil","stem":"Which work is a poetry collection rather than a novel?","choices":["The Trial","Animal Farm","The Magic Mountain","The Man Without Qualities","The Flowers of Evil"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Baudelaire's The Flowers of Evil is a collection of poems; the other works are novels.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"E","contentFingerprint":"3cfbe5513c7ff3c3cf7b745f2fd4932ea2df4637677e3361e4e8a3929aa1ef3a"},{"qid":847,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q847","major":"History & Politics","era":"Contemporary","label":"Italian constitutional forms of direct democracy","stem":"Which instrument is NOT provided for by the Italian Constitution in the same way as the others listed?","choices":["Abrogative referendum","Constitutional referendum","Popular legislative initiative","Propositive referendum","Petition to Parliament"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Constitution provides for the other listed instruments, but not a general national propositive referendum.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"ea9f811b8a9130f4dca813500d4972a337357e6638139bedbb050f394f337f6f"},{"qid":848,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q848","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Modern","label":"To Kill a Mockingbird","stem":"Who wrote To Kill a Mockingbird?","choices":["Georges Simenon","Iris Murdoch","Elena Ferrante","Harper Lee","Ernest Hemingway"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Harper Lee published To Kill a Mockingbird in 1960.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"fccbe0793a0fee2c0d4214566ca9bfb1073d7c60b94a18b8a8431bd34537df52"},{"qid":849,"id":"ALPHA-B009-Q849","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"The Spanish Civil War","stem":"During the Spanish Civil War, Franco's forces fought primarily against which side?","choices":["The Republicans, supported by Communists","The Christian Democrats","The Fascist Party","The Democratic Party","The Liberal Democratic Party"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Franco's Nationalists fought the Republican coalition, which included Communists and several other political groups.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3514.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"78ad39ba8d7a32b4e96e3594858a0e8cfff3f66f98542d7e9c3f186b8b6581f9"},{"qid":850,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q850","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Pontine Marshes reclamation","stem":"The reclamation of the Pontine Marshes, carried out as part of the extensive programme of “integral land reclamation”, took place:","choices":["During the First World War","In the mid-1930s","In the 1960s","In the mid-1950s","After the 1970s"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The major reclamation programme and the foundation of new towns in the Pontine area took place under the Fascist regime in the 1930s.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourcePage":91,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"931cba46976b8f7f8732c782f48e72f2c977b0154d9e008c6ffb0027f7d10908"},{"qid":851,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q851","major":"History & Politics","era":"Medieval","label":"European dynasties","stem":"Which of the following pairings of a dynasty and a European country is incorrect?","choices":["Bourbon — Spain","Lancaster — Germany","Tudor — England","Valois — France","Romanov — Russia"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The House of Lancaster was an English royal dynasty, not a German one.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourcePage":91,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"818a832abcd8296826ddb54d060b5dcbec334d2dcd1e2e90bc1a2b7dd948ad64"},{"qid":852,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q852","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Impressionism and Expressionism","stem":"Identify the incorrect statement about the differences between Impressionism and Expressionism.","choices":["The Impressionist artist limits attention to the interaction between light and the eye; Expressionism instead takes an inward, deeper view of the human soul.","Impressionism is consistently marked by a positive attitude to life; the underlying character of Expressionist work remains profoundly dramatic.","The Impressionist artist readily uses “ugly” and unpleasant images; Expressionism offers images of unquestionable beauty, with subjects chosen solely to illustrate the joy of living.","Impressionism seeks to represent reality with a new sensitivity; Expressionist painting is wholly anti-naturalistic.","Impressionism seeks beauty and portrays life through pleasant moments lived carefree; in Expressionism, the “ugly” becomes a genuine aesthetic category."],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Option C reverses the contrast: Expressionism often uses distortion and unsettling subjects to communicate inner experience.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourcePage":91,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"f28974b0ebe03c39c30b30762bf73e4ce8522fe7f9424b1af5afada5492b4753"},{"qid":853,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q853","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","label":"Iron construction","stem":"At the beginning of the nineteenth century, the possibility of constructing buildings with large spaces and relatively small load-bearing structures was due to:","choices":["The improvement of traditional masonry construction methods","The introduction of cast iron and iron","The discovery of reinforced concrete","The abandonment of marble","The improvement of wood-processing techniques"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Iron and cast-iron structural members enabled larger spans and lighter supports than traditional load-bearing masonry.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourcePage":91,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"2b9a9878d3490ac3075ede276cfc9a0453cb229781ffce03a94affb970262dd8"},{"qid":854,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q854","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Romanesque groin vault bay","stem":"In a Romanesque cathedral, the space defined by a groin-vaulted bay is structurally characterised by:","choices":["Four arches","Six arches","Eight arches","Twelve arches","A variable number of arches, from three to six"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The source counts the four boundary arches of the bay. The diagonal intersections are groins, not additional boundary arches.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourcePage":91,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"68be37fd637dad73345b7ff84777bf34791556c936e23c8ea2b63b405851ac53","visibleMarkDiscrepancy":{"visibleStudyMark":"A","reviewedAnswer":"B","reason":"The handwritten mark differs from the reviewed structural or historical answer."}},{"qid":855,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q855","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Warhol and Brillo","stem":"The product shown was used by a famous artist in an artwork. Who was the artist?","choices":["Andy Warhol","Roy Lichtenstein","Piet Mondrian","Mario Schifano","Jeff Koons"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Warhol's Brillo Boxes reproduce the commercial packaging of Brillo soap pads as a Pop Art subject.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourcePage":91,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[655,401,923,683]],"method":"source_crop","width":268,"height":282,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"7281e90c9c7b418edb4cca20d2b7f9eda4388ed11d72b9d87efaf644813b4a56","visualSubject":"Warhol and Brillo","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":856,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q856","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","label":"Architects and patrons matching","stem":"Choose the alternative that correctly matches each work to its patron.\n1. Stupinigi hunting lodge (Juvarra)\n2. Rimini Cathedral (Leon Battista Alberti)\n3. Royal Palace of Caserta (Vanvitelli)\n4. La Sacra Famiglia (Michelangelo)\na. Victor Amadeus II of Savoy\nb. Doni family\nc. Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta\nd. Charles III of Bourbon","choices":["1–a; 2–c; 3–d; 4–b","1–b; 2–c; 3–a; 4–d","1–a; 2–b; 3–d; 4–c","1–c; 2–a; 3–d; 4–b","1–a; 2–b; 3–c; 4–d"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Here Sacra Famiglia means Michelangelo's Holy Family, the Doni Tondo, commissioned by the Doni family. It does not mean Gaudí's Sagrada Família basilica. The other patrons are Victor Amadeus II, Malatesta and Charles of Bourbon.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourcePage":91,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.uffizi.it/en/artworks/holy-family-known-as-the-doni-tondo"],"contentFingerprint":"51b253de28579b2213bbe12b61ac62fb832f3f03afc46cf09ebd845e990ca665"},{"qid":857,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q857","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Giotto campanile plan","stem":"Giotto's Campanile in Florence has which plan?","choices":["Square, with polygonal corner buttresses","Octagonal polygon, with circular corner reinforcements","A combination of straight and curved lines","Circular","Hexagonal"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Florentine campanile has a square plan and strengthened polygonal corners.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3591.jpeg","sourcePage":91,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"96b33d30cde2a981bd5065398dd1d6b0bdb84dbba6992fb43f08a75a5f55aa84"},{"qid":858,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q858","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Groin vault construction","stem":"Which of the following statements about a groin vault is false?","choices":["It is obtained by the intersection of four barrel vaults.","It is formed by four webs.","It is typical of Romanesque architecture.","It is structurally characterised by round arches.","If its arches are pointed, it is called a Gothic groin vault."],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A groin vault is formed by two intersecting barrel vaults, not four.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"0afb5551c23fd602555a60adf981bfcf3c1923d29fea284d420098236c88cb04"},{"qid":859,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q859","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Arch diagram terminology","stem":"Match each numbered arch diagram to its correct name.\na. Rampant arch\nb. Ogee or flame arch\nc. Pointed arch\nd. Round arch","choices":["1–c; 2–d; 3–b; 4–a","1–a; 2–d; 3–b; 4–c","1–d; 2–a; 3–c; 4–b","1–c; 2–a; 3–d; 4–b","1–d; 2–b; 3–c; 4–a"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The diagrams show a pointed arch, a semicircular arch, an ogee arch and an asymmetric rampant arch, respectively.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[253,456,377,561],[429,465,551,560],[253,581,376,691],[427,587,550,691]],"method":"numbered_source_crop_composite","width":480,"height":310,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"4189be7fb06be4d6d57d195d333616b1a63f2b0352128d945aec4b5593a90dd9","visualSubject":"Arch diagram terminology","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use an original labelled schematic preserving geometry for generated variants."},{"qid":860,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q860","major":"Architecture","era":"20th century","label":"Triennale Milano building","stem":"The Triennale is housed in a building called:","choices":["Palazzo della Ragione","Palazzo Te","Palazzo dell'Arte","Palazzo del Design","Palazzo Reale"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Triennale Milano occupies the Palazzo dell'Arte, designed by Giovanni Muzio and opened in 1933.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://triennale.org/en/about/history-and-mission"],"contentFingerprint":"e96145223b4313813adfe66a80d6e5925c4aba12063e173e4b13c1183be41c43"},{"qid":861,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q861","major":"History & Politics","era":"Medieval","label":"Charlemagne coronation","stem":"Charlemagne was crowned emperor in the year 800 by which pope?","choices":["John I","Victor V","Leo III","Leo I","Pius II"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne in Rome on Christmas Day in 800.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"229d6de715d0f17661e2a6407c858292335692fd5fbb6c746f4ba01ba1bc09c1"},{"qid":862,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q862","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Flamenco origin","stem":"Flamenco is a form of music and dance characteristic of which country?","choices":["Greece","Brazil","Argentina","Spain","Portugal"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Flamenco developed in Spain, particularly Andalusia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"73306b74f02d9e702bfb70eb245ecd195b7774a7acf1adf939a1b67875eb09c6"},{"qid":863,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q863","major":"Language & Literature","era":"19th century","label":"Pascoli Il fanciullino","stem":"Who wrote the essay Il fanciullino?","choices":["G. Carducci","G. D'Annunzio","G. Pascoli","G. Verga","C. Collodi"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Giovanni Pascoli's Il fanciullino sets out his view of the childlike perception underlying poetry.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"b2a441a64f743a107639a1929de370bfb969c489ddc5f6f779c95706541684e5"},{"qid":864,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q864","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Paris museums","stem":"Which of the following is not a Paris museum?","choices":["Madame Tussauds","Musée du quai Branly","Louvre","Musée Grévin","Musée d'Orsay"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Paris wax museum in this list is Musée Grévin. Madame Tussauds is associated with London and other cities.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"303f56a184aa01a644d878f862129e4ca7c4b63935f83bbb8e154fca42b81b6a"},{"qid":865,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q865","major":"Art History","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Primary colour models","stem":"Which of these is a primary colour in the additive RGB colour model?","choices":["Cyan","White","Black","Red","Magenta"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Red, green and blue are the additive primaries. Cyan and magenta are subtractive primaries in a different model; specifying RGB leaves one correct option.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"bec9ba4842d319af39e496a6f2250c6012374e135b36850ace5117b3cf6030d8","originalRecord":{"qid":865,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q865","major":"Art History","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Primary colour models","stem":"Which of the following is a primary colour?","choices":["Cyan","White","Black","Red","Magenta"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"The source intends red in the traditional red–yellow–blue model, but cyan and magenta are also primaries in the subtractive CMY model. The question needs a specified colour model before it can be graded fairly.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The colour model is unspecified: red, cyan and magenta can each be primary in different models.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"c73ecff60851909c5a07624059804bafb584c2a0e2c35cf573b9f8aaaa432b29"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"D"},{"qid":866,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q866","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Italian Constitution asylum","stem":"Complete the following text with the correct terms.\n“The … (1) status of foreigners is regulated by law in conformity with international rules and treaties. A foreigner who is prevented in their own country from effectively … (2) the democratic freedoms guaranteed by the Italian Constitution has the right of … (3) in the territory of the Republic, under the conditions established by law.”\n(Italian Constitution, Article 10)","choices":["1. Political; 2. using; 3. free movement","1. Social; 2. satisfying; 3. voting","1. Legal; 2. exercising; 3. asylum","1. Economic; 2. enjoying; 3. citizenship","1. Family; 2. using; 3. marriage"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Article 10 refers to legal status, the exercise of democratic freedoms and the right of asylum.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.senato.it/istituzione/la-costituzione/principi-fondamentali/articolo-10"],"contentFingerprint":"550d000a95c77502646ea9d95def82eb22cd4787964b06e06abf574de0ec1371"},{"qid":867,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q867","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Gaddafi Libya","stem":"Which of the following was Libya's highest authority for 42 years?","choices":["Ruhollah Khomeini","Osama bin Laden","Muammar Gaddafi","Saddam Hussein","Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Muammar Gaddafi ruled Libya from 1969 until 2011, a period of approximately 42 years.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"c37ced0decdf50c7a400599bce6af6d7dba572b9bf95c5029df79bc3ce22e405"},{"qid":868,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q868","major":"Language & Literature","era":"20th century","label":"The Great Gatsby author","stem":"Who wrote the novel The Great Gatsby?","choices":["Mark Twain","Ernest M. Hemingway","John F. Steinbeck","Francis S. Fitzgerald","William C. Faulkner"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"F. Scott Fitzgerald wrote The Great Gatsby, published in 1925.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3592.jpeg","sourcePage":92,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"8060633652a4d38a316cdd6e3f352be88462758fab809363ce77ec4e9b64a2c9"},{"qid":869,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q869","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Sinn Féin and Ireland","stem":"Sinn Féin, an independence and republican party founded in 1905, refers to which of these historically disputed geographical areas?","choices":["Malta","Cyprus","Corsica","Falkland Islands","Ireland"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Sinn Féin is an Irish republican political movement whose history concerns Ireland and Irish independence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"015823094a0f5e019a8bff2caebd3f5bb0bb37703895a18e262031c4a137d1a6"},{"qid":870,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q870","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Six-Day War outcome","stem":"The 1967 war between Israel and a coalition of Arab countries, known as the Six-Day War, ended with:","choices":["An Israeli victory","The deployment of UN peacekeepers","An armistice imposed by the USA and USSR","An Arab victory","An armistice mediated by NATO"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Israel won the June 1967 war against Egypt, Jordan and Syria.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"50320b74acf58413826d0907be2567f4176285cd9a4e272d3d5c52e874b4a563"},{"qid":871,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q871","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Arches and architectural styles","stem":"Choose the alternative that correctly associates each type of arch with its characteristic architectural style.","choices":["Pointed — Gothic; round — Rococo; ogee — Moorish; rampant — Romanesque","Pointed — Romanesque; round — Romanesque; ogee — Gothic; rampant — Gothic","Pointed — Gothic; round — Romanesque; ogee — Gothic; rampant — Gothic","Pointed — Romanesque; round — Romanesque; ogee — Gothic; rampant — Moorish","Pointed — Baroque; round — Rococo; ogee — Moorish; rampant — Gothic"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Pointed, ogee and rampant arches occur in Gothic architecture; the round arch is characteristic of Romanesque architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"07f3d8d60dfe5f3e47b4b2078cb250ca29dae77e2c31f6bd488925a43ca1e733"},{"qid":872,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q872","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"I Have a Dream speech","stem":"“I have a dream.” Washington, 28 August 1963. What was the dream referred to?","choices":["The end of the Korean War","The granting of a constitution","The end of racial discrimination","The “New Frontier” of American politics","The end of slavery"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Martin Luther King Jr.'s speech called for racial equality and an end to segregation and discrimination.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"df8545454c6bbb4cec60567ecb5f2efa6d5bea9944205338d17a0b7dc5284464"},{"qid":873,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q873","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","label":"Treaty of Paris Cuba","stem":"Under the 1898 Treaty of Paris, which country occupied Cuba after Spain relinquished sovereignty?","choices":["Brazil","France","The United States","Portugal","Spain"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The United States occupied Cuba after Spain relinquished sovereignty in 1898. Calling Cuba a protectorate directly created by that treaty conflates later political arrangements.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"8bae8e22f1c4771542d8a8624547e459265cf25253f444300c07b09cfecbe106","originalRecord":{"qid":873,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q873","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","label":"Treaty of Paris Cuba","stem":"The Treaty of Paris signed in 1898 reduced Cuba to a protectorate of which country?","choices":["Brazil","France","The United States","Portugal","Spain"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"The Spanish–American War ended in 1898 and Spain relinquished Cuba. Subsequent US occupation and the Platt Amendment established US control; protectorate status was not itself a clause of the 1898 treaty.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The treaty date is correct, but Cuba protectorate status conflates the 1898 treaty with later US control.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"1f12677dd7f85b80a25190066f6923b852e25d1c52f197078b1796bd374cfee3"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"C"},{"qid":874,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q874","major":"Architecture","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Parts of a masonry arch","stem":"Match letters A, B, C and D in the diagram to the elements they indicate.","choices":["A: keystone; B: voussoirs; C: springer or impost stone; D: pier","A: pier; B: springer or impost stone; C: voussoirs; D: keystone","A: springer or impost stone; B: keystone; C: voussoirs; D: pier","A: keystone; B: pier; C: voussoirs; D: springer or impost stone","A: keystone; B: springer or impost stone; C: voussoirs; D: pier"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"A is the crown keystone, B the springing stone, C the wedge-shaped voussoirs, and D the supporting pier.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[595,195,902,403]],"method":"source_crop_with_note_redaction","width":307,"height":208,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"ad20c36a410a4d37dd831835753e32b4899cc781e858a7cde286696928cbd5cf","visualSubject":"Parts of a masonry arch","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use an original labelled schematic preserving geometry for generated variants."},{"qid":875,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q875","major":"Architecture","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Golden section","stem":"The expression “golden section” refers to:","choices":["The gold-plated decoration of a Baroque building","A technical drawing in which an object or building is cut by a vertical plane to reveal its internal features","A geometric proportion dividing a segment into two parts in a harmonious ratio","The chamber of a Greek temple where the deity's statue has a privileged position","None of the other answers"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"In the golden ratio, the whole divided by the longer part equals the longer part divided by the shorter part, approximately 1.618.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"e566d67ec4a83ad20b5aea8ecd1755e8607d0ae92fb4a0261d2807336fc2a1e5"},{"qid":876,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q876","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","label":"Young Italy founder","stem":"Young Italy was founded by:","choices":["Giuseppe Garibaldi","Carlo Cattaneo","Ciro Menotti","Giuseppe Mazzini","Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Giuseppe Mazzini founded Giovine Italia in 1831 to promote a united, independent Italian republic.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"7700b067822eeb49c7013a7bbd81c74563f9b299f6559cdd687c54c13a677d02"},{"qid":877,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q877","major":"Architecture","era":"20th century","label":"Modulor inventor","stem":"Who devised the Modulor, a notable application of the golden section as a system for proportioning spaces intended for human life?","choices":["Leon Battista Alberti","Le Corbusier","Giò Ponti","Leonardo da Vinci","Giotto"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Le Corbusier developed the Modulor using human measurements and the golden ratio.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3593.jpeg","sourcePage":93,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"ac937b450d46b5aa1b28911b1ba3b29d095defada30a54951e9cc93e2ee2a5b4"},{"qid":878,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q878","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Building sections chronology","stem":"Identify the correct chronological order, from oldest to newest, of the buildings whose sections are shown.","choices":["3; 4; 2; 1; 5","4; 2; 3; 5; 1","4; 2; 5; 3; 1","4; 3; 2; 1; 5","3; 4; 2; 5; 1"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The sequence is the Roman Pantheon (3), a Romanesque church (4), a Gothic church (2), Florence Cathedral's Renaissance dome (5), and St Peter's Renaissance dome in Rome (1).","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3594.jpeg","sourcePage":94,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3594.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[236,141,443,380],[224,405,474,655],[235,676,519,866],[249,894,484,1039],[230,1054,478,1321]],"method":"numbered_source_crop_composite","width":700,"height":936,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"a9de3d715ea5dd2516891192ed2d032e08dc146eb1fd3f52196fdd27786b9f6d","visualSubject":"Building sections chronology","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use an original labelled schematic preserving geometry for generated variants."},{"qid":879,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q879","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"Artists chronological order","stem":"Which of the proposed sequences is in the correct chronological order?","choices":["Leonardo da Vinci; Filippo Brunelleschi; Gian Lorenzo Bernini; Amedeo Modigliani; Antonio Canova","Filippo Brunelleschi; Leonardo da Vinci; Gian Lorenzo Bernini; Antonio Canova; Amedeo Modigliani","Filippo Brunelleschi; Gian Lorenzo Bernini; Leonardo da Vinci; Antonio Canova; Amedeo Modigliani","Leonardo da Vinci; Filippo Brunelleschi; Gian Lorenzo Bernini; Antonio Canova; Amedeo Modigliani","Amedeo Modigliani; Leonardo da Vinci; Filippo Brunelleschi; Gian Lorenzo Bernini; Antonio Canova"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"In birth-date order: Brunelleschi (1377), Leonardo (1452), Bernini (1598), Canova (1757), Modigliani (1884).","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3594.jpeg","sourcePage":94,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"7eab646a1d552981a9989ec9b983af27c2660a307faf84afd861c3e4d2139cf9"},{"qid":880,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q880","major":"Architecture","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Vault diagrams matching","stem":"Match each numbered drawing of a vaulted roof to its correct name.\na. Cloister vault\nb. Sail vault\nc. Groin vault\nd. Barrel vault","choices":["1–b; 2–a; 3–c; 4–d","1–d; 2–b; 3–c; 4–d","1–a; 2–d; 3–b; 4–c","1–d; 2–a; 3–b; 4–c","1–a; 2–b; 3–d; 4–c"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The drawings show a barrel vault (1), a cloister vault (2), a sail vault (3) and a groin vault (4).","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3594.jpeg","sourcePage":94,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3594.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[697,578,866,721],[900,574,1066,713],[695,748,869,914],[900,747,1072,915]],"method":"numbered_source_crop_composite_with_polygon_clip","width":500,"height":426,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"cbd5d84417c9f8eb8d4e29c423cd47518638be0c92ce8342ead4b02520628bd5","visualSubject":"Vault diagrams matching","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use an original labelled schematic preserving geometry for generated variants."},{"qid":881,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q881","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Gothic buttressing","stem":"A novelty of Gothic architecture was the disappearance of the thick masonry masses typical of Romanesque buildings. Which technical innovation made this possible?","choices":["The system of external buttresses","The reduction in building height","The introduction of cast iron and iron","The introduction of reinforced concrete","The use of clustered piers instead of circular or square piers"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"External buttresses and flying buttresses carry vault thrust outside the main walls, allowing thinner walls and larger windows.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3594.jpeg","sourcePage":94,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"555181688d168a92667987015c27ebcf5e9c780dfe32e06dbae22916a666ea84"},{"qid":882,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q882","major":"Architecture","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Roof truss definition","stem":"In architecture, a roof truss is:","choices":["A vaulted construction covering a circular, polygonal or elliptical space","In churches, the space between four piers beneath a groin vault","An architectural structure formed by three beams arranged as an isosceles triangle","An element forming the structure of a vault or dome, dividing its surface and carrying thrust to supporting piers","A window divided vertically into two parts by a small column or pier and surmounted by an arch"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A simple triangular truss has two sloping rafters and a horizontal tie beam; it supports a roof.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourcePage":95,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"0c0a84990ed3b0d0fd04cb2d78a31ac7f7b401a168b66d17f6c3803a1bb10b6b"},{"qid":883,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q883","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Greek temple prostyle plan","stem":"The temple shown in the plan is:","choices":["Pseudoperipteral","Prostyle","In antis","Peripteral","Dipteral"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"A prostyle temple has a row of columns across its front, without a surrounding colonnade.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourcePage":95,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[258,463,351,598]],"method":"source_crop","width":93,"height":135,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"545e764db2cceeef24d75a729d348e141c2f6c5ad85e22c19e1000d532a0c188","visualSubject":"Greek temple prostyle plan","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use an original labelled schematic preserving geometry for generated variants."},{"qid":884,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q884","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Last president of the USSR","stem":"Which of these Russian political figures was President of the USSR when it dissolved?","choices":["Putin","Lenin","Brezhnev","Gorbachev","Yeltsin"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Mikhail Gorbachev was President of the Soviet Union until its dissolution in December 1991.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourcePage":95,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"9c51578c95e0ebfb4a3755e6eb0303c07a471117a9898780c7e22d0e592df79a"},{"qid":885,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q885","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Monet Impression Sunrise","stem":"The Claude Monet painting shown:","choices":["Is the most representative painting of Romanticism","Was painted in two other versions under different lighting conditions","Inspired a critic to give Impressionism its name","Caused a scandal at the Salon des Refusés","Is a painting of social protest"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Monet's Impression, Sunrise prompted Louis Leroy's satirical review of the 1874 exhibition and the term Impressionism.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourcePage":95,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[181,1033,448,1239]],"method":"source_crop","width":267,"height":206,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"7069d4f6301bc5c1e1bb1cbd3484af6c1cda97feed10212f1b3890bb17ae5c56","visualSubject":"Monet Impression Sunrise","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":886,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q886","major":"Art History","era":"18th century","label":"David Oath of the Horatii","stem":"Identify the title and artist of the work shown.","choices":["The Intervention of the Sabine Women — Jacques-Louis David","The Oath of the Horatii — Jacques-Louis David","Ruggiero Rescuing Angelica — Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","The Miracle of Saint Mark Freeing the Slave — Jacopo Tintoretto","The Oath of the Horatii — Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Jacques-Louis David painted The Oath of the Horatii in 1784; its disciplined composition embodies Neoclassicism.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourcePage":95,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[595,118,957,387]],"method":"source_crop","width":362,"height":269,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"79671ce4fcf2ebc141e764fc90894b8f17aa703660c141f2380dd60c803492f0","visualSubject":"David Oath of the Horatii","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":887,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q887","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Ingres Turkish Bath ideal","stem":"Which ideal of beauty did Ingres draw on when painting the twenty-five nude women in The Turkish Bath?","choices":["His first wife, Madeleine Chapelle","His second wife, Delphine Ramel","Manet's Olympia","Botticelli's Venus","Raphael's La Fornarina"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Ingres admired Raphael and reused the idealised female beauty associated with La Fornarina in his treatment of the nude.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourcePage":95,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"6c88b107cce42e5e36dce27ea59ef03121848ebb9caa54e21fff8ac5a3e71e86"},{"qid":888,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q888","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Stylobate identification","stem":"Which of the labelled elements is the stylobate?","choices":["a","b","c","d","e"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The stylobate is the upper platform or step on which the temple's columns stand, labelled e.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourcePage":95,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3596.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[],"method":"clean_redraw_from_source_diagram","essential":true},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"c26816b1cd59527a3c3f0dbc6d72bf47c6f98d50429ac04863e1ad2d9d0f517d","visualSubject":"Stylobate identification","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use an original labelled schematic preserving geometry for generated variants.","scoringKey":"E"},{"qid":889,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q889","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Famous partners matching","stem":"Match these pairs of life partners, whether married or not.\n1. Marilyn Monroe; 2. Anne Boleyn; 3. Nilde Jotti; 4. Giuseppina Strepponi; 5. Drusilla Tanzi; 6. Xanthippe; 7. Cosima Liszt\na. Eugenio Montale; b. Palmiro Togliatti; c. Arthur Miller; d. Giuseppe Verdi; e. Henry VIII; f. Socrates; g. Richard Wagner","choices":["1–c; 2–e; 3–a; 4–d; 5–f; 6–b; 7–g","1–g; 2–e; 3–c; 4–a; 5–b; 6–f; 7–d","1–c; 2–e; 3–a; 4–b; 5–g; 6–f; 7–d","1–c; 2–e; 3–b; 4–d; 5–a; 6–f; 7–g","1–b; 2–g; 3–c; 4–a; 5–d; 6–f; 7–e"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The partners are Monroe–Arthur Miller, Boleyn–Henry VIII, Jotti–Togliatti, Strepponi–Verdi, Tanzi–Montale, Xanthippe–Socrates and Cosima–Wagner. The source's Henry Miller is a name error.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"3721b78d60aa6635aa1860266047867559126ef1ab34059aa66bdb32a682582a","originalRecord":{"qid":889,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q889","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Famous partners matching","stem":"Match these pairs of life partners, whether married or not.\n1. Marilyn Monroe; 2. Anne Boleyn; 3. Nilde Jotti; 4. Giuseppina Strepponi; 5. Drusilla Tanzi; 6. Xanthippe; 7. Cosima Liszt\na. Eugenio Montale; b. Palmiro Togliatti; c. Henry Miller; d. Giuseppe Verdi; e. Henry VIII; f. Socrates; g. Richard Wagner","choices":["1–c; 2–e; 3–a; 4–d; 5–f; 6–b; 7–g","1–g; 2–e; 3–c; 4–a; 5–b; 6–f; 7–d","1–c; 2–e; 3–a; 4–b; 5–g; 6–f; 7–d","1–c; 2–e; 3–b; 4–d; 5–a; 6–f; 7–g","1–b; 2–g; 3–c; 4–a; 5–d; 6–f; 7–e"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"The source prints Henry Miller, but Marilyn Monroe married Arthur Miller. The intended complete mapping is otherwise option D. This printed-name error must be resolved before grading the source question.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The printed option says Henry Miller; Marilyn Monroe married Arthur Miller.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"7815701b04aa1f8fb89664086ba67b8f07787cbc8ff95e03dcdf5f994f02ba7a"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"D"},{"qid":890,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q890","major":"Science & Tech","era":"20th century","label":"Bohr Nobel Prize","stem":"Which physicist won the Nobel Prize in 1922 for studies of atomic structure?","choices":["Niels H. D. Bohr","Joseph J. Thomson","Ernest Rutherford","Max Planck","Robert A. Millikan"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Niels Bohr received the 1922 Nobel Prize in Physics for investigating atomic structure and the radiation emitted by atoms.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1922/summary/1000/"],"contentFingerprint":"2f9ba6a3073b350a92acc293dfa6e116ee8e17678adea5c218c64d83b4d3bcde"},{"qid":891,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q891","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","label":"Historical contemporaries","stem":"Which of these people could not have met the others?","choices":["Dante Alighieri","Niccolò Machiavelli","Giotto di Bondone","Francesco Petrarca","They were all contemporaries"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Machiavelli lived from 1469 to 1527, long after Dante, Giotto and Petrarch.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"fac0239505679a86e7452357b8c3f8078fe6a3f4c374809e17132bf699119235"},{"qid":892,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q892","major":"History & Politics","era":"17th century","label":"Galileo condemnation","stem":"Galileo Galilei's heliocentric theory was condemned by:","choices":["The Order of the Medici","The Roman Senate","The Holy Office","The United Nations","The Geographic Society of London"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Roman Inquisition, also called the Holy Office, condemned Galileo in 1633.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"369b040e0e21bcfb8694a3cec995540fa56a1108344900d1e7b4fcdde9929b13"},{"qid":893,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q893","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Medieval","label":"Dante exile prophecy","stem":"“You shall leave everything most dearly loved: this is the arrow that the bow of exile shoots first. You shall learn how salt is the taste of another's bread, and how hard a path it is to go down and up another's stairs.”\nIn Canto XVII of the Paradiso, who addresses this prophecy to Dante?","choices":["Farinata","Brunetto","Oderisi","Cacciaguida","Beatrice"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Dante's ancestor Cacciaguida foretells his exile in Paradiso XVII.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"926b26453aceee34dd1f7b496310d535077af5508997bdc22a3051725f7703ef"},{"qid":894,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q894","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Van Gogh painting technique","stem":"Which of the following is not characteristic of Van Gogh's painting technique?","choices":["Striking colour combinations using complementary colours","Twisted, thick and swirling brushstrokes","Reality interpreted photographically","Immediate, vigorous drawing","Art as the expression of an inner tension"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Van Gogh transforms colour and brushwork expressively rather than reproducing reality like a photograph.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"b2bf127bb4a11490967af9ae9616077e72abcf4700db43ea706a2db70acddb85"},{"qid":895,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q895","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Modern paintings and painters","stem":"Match each artist to a work they created.\n1. Mondrian; 2. Magritte; 3. Kandinsky; 4. Van Gogh; 5. Seurat\na. Rhythm of Black Lines\nb. The Blue Rider\nc. The Potato Eaters\nd. This Is Not a Pipe\ne. A Sunday Afternoon on the Island of La Grande Jatte","choices":["1–a; 2–d; 3–b; 4–c; 5–e","1–b; 2–a; 3–c; 4–e; 5–d","1–c; 2–b; 3–a; 4–c; 5–d","1–b; 2–d; 3–a; 4–e; 5–c","1–a; 2–b; 3–c; 4–d; 5–e"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Mondrian painted Rhythm of Black Lines; Magritte, The Treachery of Images (“This is not a pipe”); Kandinsky, The Blue Rider; Van Gogh, The Potato Eaters; Seurat, La Grande Jatte.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"a23da1a27311b2a0a1b08546f19b5930d1a422ac50ef10107339aa1b325a29f9"},{"qid":896,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q896","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","label":"Art Nouveau in Spain","stem":"In Italy Art Nouveau took its name, Stile Liberty, from Arthur Lasenby Liberty's English stores. What was it called in Spain?","choices":["Jugendstil","Style Guimard","Modernismo","Expressionism","Fauves"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Spanish and Catalan branch of Art Nouveau is generally known as Modernismo or Modernisme.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"4f261e7dd02adf97a180ee979c7826bd53d3d96ac46bf1b03aad485a8d711c06"},{"qid":897,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q897","major":"Art History","era":"Ancient","label":"Sculpture chronology","stem":"What is the correct chronological order of these sculptures?\n1. Cupid and Psyche (Canova)\n2. David (Michelangelo)\n3. Development of a Bottle in Space (Boccioni)\n4. Discobolus (Myron)\n5. Apollo and Daphne (Bernini)","choices":["4; 3; 2; 5; 1","1; 2; 4; 3; 5","4; 2; 5; 1; 3","1; 3; 5; 4; 2","2; 5; 4; 1; 3"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Myron's ancient Discobolus precedes Michelangelo's David, Bernini's Apollo and Daphne, Canova's Cupid and Psyche, and Boccioni's 1913 Futurist work.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3597.jpeg","sourcePage":96,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"36f0c19f425e4090bde3dff7768cbf14ed884c90571a555f041c6d09b7e844c5"},{"qid":898,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q898","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"Creation of Adam detail","stem":"Identify the famous fresco from which this detail is taken, and its artist.","choices":["The School of Athens — Raphael","The Last Judgement — Michelangelo","The Flood — Michelangelo","The Last Judgement — Raphael","The Creation of Adam — Michelangelo"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The nearly touching hands belong to Michelangelo's Creation of Adam on the Sistine Chapel ceiling.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[190,132,442,299]],"method":"source_crop","width":252,"height":167,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"7403ebad0ce11d3e7c78d24cdb91cab423c5f0bcb54836240c836cd50ed19460","visualSubject":"Creation of Adam detail","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":899,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q899","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"Renaissance pigment suppliers","stem":"In the Renaissance, a painter could buy colours:","choices":["At an apothecary's shop","At a military armoury","At a dyer's shop","At the town hall","At a church"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Renaissance pigments could be bought from apothecaries and related suppliers. The overlapping English synonym pharmacist is replaced by a distinct distractor.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"b832248f88e9ba48c4a8adc28f621c6224dac92df375cdc8630589d2c0293452","originalRecord":{"qid":899,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q899","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"Renaissance pigment suppliers","stem":"In the Renaissance, a painter could buy colours:","choices":["At an apothecary's shop","At a pharmacist's shop","At a dyer's shop","At the town hall","At a church"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"The source distinguishes the historical speziale, a seller of drugs and pigments, from a modern pharmacist. In English, apothecary and pharmacist can overlap, so the translated alternatives need review.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"Apothecary and pharmacist overlap in English; the translated distractors are not sufficiently distinct.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"5c4b711337112d6838aa744ee9c9c7fd1553062bf9ac668d9e60174d697ceeae"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"A"},{"qid":900,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q900","major":"Architecture","era":"20th century","label":"Le Corbusier five points","stem":"Which of these is not one of Le Corbusier's five points of a new architecture?","choices":["The square module","The roof garden","The ribbon window","Pilotis","The free plan"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The five points are pilotis, the free plan, the free façade, ribbon windows and the roof garden; a square module is not one of them.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"e99bf62312cddf54c079c680ca78ba5ce8ceca4cd30c704312d1b96f20a61804"},{"qid":901,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q901","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Guernica palette","stem":"The principal colours of Picasso's Guernica are:","choices":["Primary colours","Secondary colours","Black, grey and white","Yellow, orange and red","Green, light blue and blue"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Guernica uses a monochrome palette of black, white and shades of grey.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"59103e1ef999213a6477af15a7878f813ff7c45668ebe7f6486eda02b8c8ffc4"},{"qid":902,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q902","major":"Design","era":"20th century","label":"Kartell Bookworm product","stem":"The Kartell product shown is:","choices":["A bookcase","A sculpture","A coat rack","A lamp","A toy"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The flexible Bookworm shelving system, designed by Ron Arad for Kartell, is a bookcase.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[166,1141,518,1305]],"method":"source_crop","width":352,"height":164,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":["https://www.kartell.com/US/designer/ron-arad/designer_4"],"contentFingerprint":"837e63188233312ff6d19de42561e160336007206e56891738ec623a6f426ad0","visualSubject":"Kartell Bookworm product","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":903,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q903","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Klimt Judith recognition","stem":"Which artist created the work shown?","choices":["Henri Matisse","Andy Warhol","Joan Miró","Gustav Klimt","Edvard Munch"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The image shows Klimt's Judith I, recognisable by its decorative gold background and the portrait's pose.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[711,125,841,357]],"method":"source_crop","width":130,"height":232,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"912708c5660aa8772c97698cbc2108e4c67d26e64ee203f17cb204b0d015999e","visualSubject":"Klimt Judith recognition","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":904,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q904","major":"Architecture","era":"20th century","label":"Pompidou architectural style","stem":"The Centre Pompidou in Paris, designed by Renzo Piano and Richard Rogers, is a leading example of which architectural movement?","choices":["High-tech","Deconstructivism","Neo-expressionism","Rationalism","Postmodernism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Its exposed structure and externally displayed service systems make the Centre Pompidou a landmark of High-tech architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"2715a8c27815dc284c14c89819028cfe0ec2aec1ba02d54894a761a948064e57"},{"qid":905,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q905","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","label":"Eiffel Tower structural engineering","stem":"The Eiffel Tower is an emblematic building of which movement in architectural history?","choices":["High-tech","Structural engineering","Art Nouveau","Expressionism","Rationalism"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Eiffel Tower demonstrates nineteenth-century iron construction and structural engineering.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"e38a9dede4cce617488fa1170b3c67041b646623fbc18d92fa4f11a7c1b3a312"},{"qid":906,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q906","major":"Architecture","era":"20th century","label":"Modern buildings and movements","stem":"Match each building to its architectural movement.\n1. Centre Pompidou, Paris\n2. Crystal Palace, London\n3. Casa Batlló, Barcelona\n4. Unité d'Habitation, Marseille\n5. Ronchamp Chapel\na. Rationalism\nb. High-tech\nc. Stile Liberty (Art Nouveau)\nd. Neo-expressionism\ne. Structural engineering","choices":["1–b; 2–e; 3–c; 4–a; 5–d","1–a; 2–b; 3–c; 4–d; 5–e","1–e; 2–a; 3–d; 4–b; 5–c","1–e; 2–b; 3–d; 4–c; 5–a","1–b; 2–e; 3–d; 4–a; 5–c"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The exposed services of Pompidou indicate High-tech; Crystal Palace represents iron-and-glass engineering; Casa Batlló is Art Nouveau; Unité is Rationalist; Ronchamp is classified here as Neo-expressionist.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3598.jpeg","sourcePage":97,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"9d3297328009fc015c94483f9c133f8b31aea616baad83b0893e57f5e1606805"},{"qid":907,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q907","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","label":"Art Nouveau names","stem":"Art Nouveau took different names in different European countries. Which of these names was never used for it?","choices":["Jugendstil","Modern Style","Stile Liberty","Stile floreale","Arte nuova"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Jugendstil, Modern Style, Stile Liberty and Stile floreale are established names for Art Nouveau; Arte nuova is not the standard historical name in this list.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"8b8d7e26e2d5c999d6250c36d2803bde42a785d0be98083da9d42dd658232bc6"},{"qid":908,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q908","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Kennedy Berlin speech","stem":"Who delivered the historic phrase “Ich bin ein Berliner” (“I am a Berliner”)?","choices":["Dwight (Ike) Eisenhower in Berlin in 1945","Frederick II of Prussia in Königsberg in 1743","John Kennedy in Berlin in 1963","Herr Georg Kaplan in Riccione in 1972","Willy Brandt in Berlin in 1961"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"President John F. Kennedy delivered the speech in West Berlin on 26 June 1963.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"7d9d6c918af544327e4d3be66ac69ec229cef7195082612765d61568e2998b82"},{"qid":909,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q909","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","label":"Buildings outside Florence","stem":"Which of these buildings is not in Florence?","choices":["Pazzi Chapel","Laurentian Library","Basilica of Santa Maria Novella","Church of Santa Maria delle Grazie","Palazzo della Signoria"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Santa Maria delle Grazie associated with Bramante and Leonardo's Last Supper is in Milan. The other listed monuments are in Florence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"9c7e402b2a15347d8dd91196410b5fe71c92b95b0bc81ae60e5692ab954a3922"},{"qid":910,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q910","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Buildings chronological order","stem":"In what chronological order were the following buildings constructed?\na. Basilica of Sant'Ambrogio, Milan\nb. Palladio's Villa Malcontenta\nc. Bramante's Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan\nd. Galleria Vittorio Emanuele, Milan\ne. Gropius's Bauhaus, Dessau","choices":["a; c; b; d; e","a; b; c; d; e","c; b; a; e; d","b; a; c; d; e","c; a; b; d; e"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Sant'Ambrogio precedes Bramante's late-fifteenth-century work, Palladio's sixteenth-century villa, the nineteenth-century Galleria and the 1920s Bauhaus.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"b456c7052019eb21a7f7caf3dd9e9a58e4122ed17ab0ba1b768741e5536136ef"},{"qid":911,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q911","major":"Science & Tech","era":"19th century","label":"Volta electric generator","stem":"Who made the first static generator of electrical energy?","choices":["Luigi Galvani","Michael Faraday","André-Marie Ampère","Alessandro Volta","Charles-Augustin de Coulomb"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The source uses static generator to refer to a non-moving electrochemical source: Volta's pile, the first battery. It does not refer to an electrostatic friction machine.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://catalogo.museogalileo.it/multimedia/ElettromagnetismoBis.html"],"contentFingerprint":"3975053f58a887ee78f013aa23f45b93c29ce1f161ae478d171001ebad6ae0b5"},{"qid":912,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q912","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Māori homeland","stem":"The Māori are a people native to which country?","choices":["Sri Lanka","New Zealand","Jamaica","Madagascar","Cuba"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Māori are the Indigenous Polynesian people of Aotearoa New Zealand.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"bbd38db71a09c3a7e2748ea632a3a99d1ba2a5a5f03733cc5c2024d6be1dddf5"},{"qid":913,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q913","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Battle of Alesia","stem":"Who fought each other at the Battle of Alesia?","choices":["Julius Caesar and Pompey","Julius Caesar and Vercingetorix","Octavian and Antony","Themistocles and Alcibiades","Octavian and Cleopatra"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"In 52 BCE Caesar besieged the Gallic forces led by Vercingetorix at Alesia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"ae2a60e346377baef1ee8137fc634f64fe79f27916793ff6e1cc3de2c9c91ffa"},{"qid":914,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q914","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"San Vitale plan","stem":"The Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna has which plan?","choices":["Central elliptical","Latin cross","Octagonal","Longitudinal","Hexagonal"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"San Vitale is an early Byzantine centrally planned church with an octagonal core.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"b9c445c302584cc8f03b4bb5e74118e15dee23c81db83b03997139b4b1e79930"},{"qid":915,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q915","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Italian synonym stigmatizzare","stem":"Which of the following is a synonym of the Italian verb stigmatizzare?","choices":["Incitare","Approvare","Condannare","Recidere","Assoggettare"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Stigmatizzare means to condemn or denounce; condannare is its synonym. The Italian lexical alternatives are preserved because they are the subject of the question.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"e23d171c620ba6f3502ce4dee28dd6878f0533bf4c0091a62af84fd4421d6d42"},{"qid":916,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q916","major":"Language & Literature","era":"18th century","label":"Mozart operas","stem":"The operas Don Giovanni and The Magic Flute were both composed by:","choices":["Puccini","Verdi","Wagner","Donizetti","Mozart"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart composed Don Giovanni (1787) and The Magic Flute (1791).","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"3e0f422078c5d1459afeca166515ed5ecfae70739006efce871e931169748d6a"},{"qid":917,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q917","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Italy land borders","stem":"Which of these countries does not share a land border with Italy?","choices":["Austria","Slovenia","Croatia","Switzerland","France"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Croatia has no land border with Italy; the other four countries do.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"708680b51eeb69495e15ad71cf564e71e1d55bc0f2bae57e9d245eab9f0f583e"},{"qid":918,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q918","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Southern hemisphere capitals","stem":"Which city is not in the Northern Hemisphere?","choices":["Mexico City","Lima","Santo Domingo","Port-au-Prince","Havana"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Lima, Peru, is south of the Equator. The other four cities are north of it.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"c105ee4bef4f3f869c91fac1b5bf38fb6d83d1beadbe4db68b8578b86648a92c"},{"qid":919,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q919","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Caryatid definition","stem":"In architecture, a caryatid is:","choices":["A support in the form of a female statue","A stepped structure","A temple doorway","A form of stone corrosion","An element of a temple roof"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A caryatid is a sculpted female figure used as an architectural support in place of a column or pier.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"436c1e9cdc8926b30c83cafb0a1e0b0793000637487d3fcd051ab358288e8fad"},{"qid":920,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q920","major":"Art History","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Traditional primary colours","stem":"The primary colours are:","choices":["White, red and black","Red, yellow and blue","Red, blue and white","White and black","Cyan, green and magenta"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The intended answer is red, yellow and blue in the traditional painter's RYB model. The source does not specify the model; modern additive primaries are red, green and blue, and subtractive primaries cyan, magenta and yellow.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_3599.jpeg","sourcePage":98,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"5d277e5844404f379a810297243e89ce1f718c0643ca7b83ee563ddb7f7cac67"},{"qid":921,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q921","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Guernica symbolism","stem":"Picasso's Guernica became a symbol of:","choices":["The sensuality of the female nude","The devouring and malicious woman","Protest against the horrors of war","The struggle for freedom of various social classes","Modern humanity's state of anguish"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Guernica responds to the bombing of the Basque town in 1937 and became a powerful anti-war image.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"2225dece7eaac7f40881e3666f6acecc65dcc45f0bd87b55f2c22dc628595bc9"},{"qid":922,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q922","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Friedrich Wanderer recognition","stem":"Who created the painting shown?","choices":["William Blake","William Turner","John Constable","Caspar David Friedrich","John Milton"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Caspar David Friedrich painted Wanderer above the Sea of Fog, a celebrated image of German Romanticism.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[210,313,412,555]],"method":"source_crop","width":202,"height":242,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"61639de2122d167ad5c366c89701b560f1cde3e677bf36fd97dee5bc077cb890","visualSubject":"Friedrich Wanderer recognition","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":923,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q923","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","label":"Charles V empire phrase","stem":"The phrase “The sun never sets on my kingdom” is said to have been spoken by:","choices":["The Great Khan addressing Marco Polo","Emperor Charles V of Habsburg","Louis XIV of France, the “Sun King”","Stadtholder Karl Gustav Åkensonn of Finland","Aleksander Nijewski after defeating the Teutonic Knights"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The expression is traditionally associated with the far-reaching Habsburg and Spanish possessions of Charles V; it is a conventional attribution, not a verified verbatim quotation.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"82b83136309f6f1767c718fb91cb9c864e889a23ff7848c6bc1cb35ae522bc18"},{"qid":924,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q924","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","label":"Cavour Crimean War troops","stem":"During the Crimean War, the Piedmontese prime minister … sent … to fight in that region.","choices":["Crispi; the Savoia Cavalry","Cavour; Folgore infantry","D'Azeglio; the San Marco battalion","Crispi; Alpini of the Julia brigade","Cavour; Bersaglieri"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Cavour sent a Sardinian-Piedmontese expeditionary force to the Crimean War in 1855; it included Bersaglieri.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"1f6122d9fa71cf1516e9143a61f0543405ce7a75ac355bc5a281c2193d7ac761"},{"qid":925,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q925","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","label":"Zanardelli penal code","stem":"In 1889 Crispi's government completed which important measure?","choices":["School reform","Reform of the armed forces","Promulgation of the Zanardelli Penal Code","Administrative reform","Support for Garibaldi's expedition to Sicily"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Zanardelli Penal Code was promulgated in 1889 and entered into force on 1 January 1890. The choice is corrected to distinguish these dates.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.normattiva.it/atto/caricaDettaglioAtto?atto.codiceRedazionale=089U6133&atto.dataPubblicazioneGazzetta=1889-06-30&qId=&tipoDettaglio=originario"],"contentFingerprint":"c6b98f0f1cff91896e5af501b48411c16d64a91a5a2cf8c7e047a0718071f1f0","originalRecord":{"qid":925,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q925","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","label":"Zanardelli penal code","stem":"In 1889 Crispi's government completed which important measure?","choices":["School reform","Reform of the armed forces","The entry into force of the Zanardelli Penal Code","Administrative reform","Support for Garibaldi's expedition to Sicily"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"The Zanardelli Code was approved and promulgated in 1889, but entered into force on 1 January 1890. The source's date and wording therefore require correction before grading.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The Zanardelli Code was promulgated in 1889 but entered into force on 1 January 1890.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.normattiva.it/atto/caricaDettaglioAtto?atto.codiceRedazionale=089U6133&atto.dataPubblicazioneGazzetta=1889-06-30&qId=&tipoDettaglio=originario"],"contentFingerprint":"ba2de3b1fe144a48cf33c95519e29369cc8d50428cb9c540220cb73b837d2545"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"C"},{"qid":926,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q926","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Goya Nude Maja recognition","stem":"Identify the artist and title of the work shown.","choices":["Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres — La Grande Odalisque","Jacques-Louis David — Madame Récamier","Francisco Goya — La maja desnuda","Titian — Venus of Urbino","Edouard Manet — Olympia"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Francisco de Goya painted The Naked Maja, showing a nude woman reclining with her arms behind her head.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[572,148,887,296]],"method":"source_crop","width":315,"height":148,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":["https://www.museodelprado.es/en/the-collection/art-work/the-nude-maja/65953b93-323e-48fe-98cb-9d4b15852b18"],"contentFingerprint":"b3125ccd51b3af777d9c26137018d17525c043ba8171004fff9647f8eeb97380","visualSubject":"Goya Nude Maja recognition","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":927,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q927","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Hayez The Kiss recognition","stem":"Who created the work shown?","choices":["Francisco Goya","Johann Heinrich Füssli","Francesco Hayez","Jean-Antoine Watteau","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Kiss, painted by Francesco Hayez in 1859, is a major work of Italian Romanticism.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[573,470,908,876]],"method":"source_crop","width":335,"height":406,"essential":true,"purpose":"Identify the depicted artwork, product, plan, section or labelled component."},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"0f8884e4818e9473a90ad0c53e50806053b384505543753d5f36d9b45b678c06","visualSubject":"Hayez The Kiss recognition","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":928,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q928","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Picasso periods chronology","stem":"Which alternative lists Picasso's artistic periods in the correct chronological order?","choices":["African period; Rose period; Blue period; Synthetic Cubism; Analytic Cubism","Rose period; Blue period; African period; Analytic Cubism; Synthetic Cubism","Blue period; Rose period; African period; Analytic Cubism; Synthetic Cubism","Synthetic Cubism; Analytic Cubism; Blue period; Rose period; African period","African period; Blue period; Rose period; Analytic Cubism; Synthetic Cubism"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Picasso's Blue and Rose periods precede the African-influenced phase, followed by Analytic and then Synthetic Cubism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_FD2C2824-F2CB-4DB5-AD02-8944C913C78C.jpeg","sourcePage":99,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"ff76f3eb9b50bace0f9bb63eec43498dfe6686f347025d24c4041b219b01d632"},{"qid":929,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q929","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Italian antonym assecondare","stem":"Which of the following is the opposite of the Italian verb assecondare?","choices":["Ascoltare","Vincere","Illudere","Avversare","Travisare"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Assecondare means to accommodate or support; avversare means to oppose. Italian alternatives are retained because this is an Italian vocabulary task.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"09bb5f2078c8b81d98b985c282683049c7f94c3dde52444185d32167ca5c5a3a"},{"qid":930,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q930","major":"Architecture","era":"Era not assigned","label":"Medallion architectural ornament","stem":"In architecture, what is meant by a medallion?","choices":["A decorative motif","A supporting surface","A spire","A cornice","A recess in a wall"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"An architectural medallion is a round or oval decorative motif, often containing a relief or portrait.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"e9a7e965397e509bc18cc55736fdbc956fac8f0d7fcd2db89a287b9f10593f3c"},{"qid":931,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q931","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Alaric sack of Rome","stem":"In what year did Alaric and the Visigoths sack Rome?","choices":["1084 CE","800 CE","410 CE","476 BCE","476 CE"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Alaric's Visigoths sacked Rome in 410 CE. The deposition of the last western Roman emperor occurred in 476 CE.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"bdbca3692e7eef3eec79bf833669a217de8b2b5150a9ef980e7d8479f6dee1b4"},{"qid":932,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q932","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Kandinsky first abstract watercolour","stem":"Who painted the work known as First Abstract Watercolour, now dated 1913 by the Centre Pompidou?","choices":["Piet Mondrian","Wassily Kandinsky","Kazimir Malevich","Paul Klee","Pablo Picasso"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The work is by Wassily Kandinsky. Although often historically associated with 1910, its owning museum currently dates it to 1913.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.centrepompidou.fr/fr/ressources/oeuvre/cMejBMj"],"contentFingerprint":"6b6a28162d4f975e63aa90ba5083c1c12a04333ae16c9fe41732fa3f021691df","originalRecord":{"qid":932,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q932","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Kandinsky first abstract watercolour","stem":"Who painted First Abstract Watercolour in 1910?","choices":["Piet Mondrian","Wassily Kandinsky","Kazimir Malevich","Paul Klee","Pablo Picasso"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"The work is by Kandinsky, but the Centre Pompidou dates it to 1913 despite its 1910 inscription. The date in the source needs correction.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Visible study marking plus independent concept review; printed answer key not supplied in this batch.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The source gives 1910; the owning museum currently dates this Kandinsky work to 1913.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.centrepompidou.fr/fr/ressources/oeuvre/cMejBMj"],"contentFingerprint":"dd52d095d3dceadb35a4c8fad7b34c6e57c3f7c601f3455cd3ab1c690a5d195c"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"B"},{"qid":933,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q933","major":"Art History","era":"Medieval","label":"Christ Pantocrator iconography","stem":"The Byzantine image of Christ Pantocrator represents:","choices":["Jesus on the cross","The infant Jesus","Jesus ascending to heaven","Jesus giving a blessing","Jesus adored by the Magi"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Christ Pantocrator is typically shown frontally, blessing with one hand and holding a Gospel book with the other.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"d3609ec7244397e2f43f18eab7ff6dd1d14b33e659b0346f74048a04a4563d52"},{"qid":934,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q934","major":"Art History","era":"18th century","label":"Canova artistic style","stem":"Antonio Canova was the foremost representative of which style?","choices":["Rococo","Mannerism","Neoclassicism","Romanticism","Art Nouveau"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Canova's idealised marble sculpture embodies the revival of classical forms associated with Neoclassicism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"345b7c22adedfb41701ac9caa31a51e0d403ed2690d07a565b840a6c80f49b0e"},{"qid":935,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q935","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Pearl Harbor attack year","stem":"In what year did Japan attack Pearl Harbor?","choices":["1940","1941","1939","1944","1938"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The attack on Pearl Harbor occurred on 7 December 1941.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"dbd2abace10af7bd63c0ad1be3657045b97e15fcab0dbf533857bb2f990c5064"},{"qid":936,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q936","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","label":"Art Nouveau regional line types","stem":"In Art Nouveau, line generates the design and acts as a dynamic element arising from a point moving in space. Match each type of line to its country of origin.\n1. Flowing line\n2. Perpendicular line\n3. Modular geometric line\n4. Magmatic line\na. Great Britain\nb. Austria\nc. France and Belgium\nd. Spain","choices":["1–b; 2–a; 3–d; 4–c","1–c; 2–a; 3–b; 4–d","1–a; 2–d; 3–b; 4–c","1–b; 2–a; 3–c; 4–d","1–c; 2–b; 3–d; 4–a"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"France and Belgium: flowing line; Great Britain: perpendicular line; Austria: modular geometric line; Spain: magmatic line. The printed answer key confirms option B.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"30d75e6a3cf93f6f1b609d556481c4bcbb54599e31e219d8f7ce28aaf6a90908"},{"qid":937,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q937","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","label":"Metaphysical painting founders","stem":"Who founded the Metaphysical school of painting?","choices":["Giorgio Morandi and Giorgio de Chirico","Carlo Carrà and Giorgio Morandi","Giorgio de Chirico and Carlo Carrà","Umberto Boccioni and Carlo Carrà","Umberto Boccioni and Giacomo Balla"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Giorgio de Chirico and Carlo Carrà are the central founders of Metaphysical painting; their association developed in Ferrara in 1917.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"9bec42ab7ee95cc3ec36413027e78364d60cb31b9a21ad1613f59cc7e4a30287"},{"qid":938,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q938","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","label":"Eclectic architecture definition","stem":"Eclecticism is an aspect of nineteenth-century European architectural culture based on:","choices":["Recovering and reworking features of earlier artistic forms through analysis of ancient monuments, often combining elements and styles superficially","Recovering the medieval model as the period in which art perfectly expressed popular feeling and manual work was considered ennobling","The relationship between structure and cladding, treated as a building's expressive “skin”","Rethinking the home as a rational “machine for living”, dimensioned to human measurements","Proposing organic architecture developing as a living form fully integrated with nature"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Eclectic architects selected and combined elements from different historical styles rather than adhering to one exclusive style.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"99ef495b4453f23f207c7e94fd7af59f602780ea1348cc082a5fb4da6ab7b9a2"},{"qid":939,"id":"ALPHA-B010-Q939","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","label":"Greek independence","stem":"In 1829, Greece freed itself from:","choices":["The French","The British","The Italians","The Turks","The Egyptians"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Greek War of Independence was fought against Ottoman rule. The 1829 Treaty of Adrianople advanced Greek autonomy; formal recognition of independence followed in 1830.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg","language":"en","version":"source-en-v38","sourceImage":"IMG_C774FF14-7BC1-4CD7-92BB-6D6EAF13708F.jpeg","sourcePage":100,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"254a799e5e96907c862e64ce9ea1af7875a4673b5366f64f0272535e70568165","visibleMarkDiscrepancy":{"visibleStudyMark":"C","reviewedAnswer":"D","reason":"The handwritten mark differs from the reviewed structural or historical answer."}},{"qid":940,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q940","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Toulouse-Lautrec and Paris cabarets","stem":"Which French artist depicted the cabarets and cafés of late nineteenth-century Paris?","choices":["Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","Édouard Manet","Edgar Degas","Gustave Courbet","Georges Seurat"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Toulouse-Lautrec is particularly associated with Montmartre nightlife and the Moulin Rouge.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"f9b92a713c0fde3886125ff2bb3908da2ba63e03a8f9180415a781efad9baf1e"},{"qid":941,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q941","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"De Chirico: The Song of Love","stem":"Identify the movement and artist of the painting shown.","choices":["Metaphysical Art — René Magritte","Dada — Man Ray","Surrealism — Giorgio de Chirico","Metaphysical Art — Giorgio de Chirico","Surrealism — René Magritte"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"De Chirico's The Song of Love combines familiar objects in an enigmatic setting characteristic of Metaphysical painting.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[210,377,470,691]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":260,"height":314},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"78eaa8bec93e9148a720d62b2b70128ca639be21f60e7e3ab3cb89222afbc630","visualSubject":"De Chirico: The Song of Love","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":942,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q942","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"Italy's entry into NATO","stem":"Which party led the Italian government when Italy joined NATO?","choices":["Republican Party","Radical Party","Socialist Party","Christian Democratic Party","Liberal Party"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Italy joined NATO in 1949 under Alcide De Gasperi, a Christian Democrat.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"9a3e267adc4ec7fe4e0aa90fbf81e4252d3d9466db47e1ea6af2f3d49baaaf31"},{"qid":943,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q943","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"The three popes of 1978","stem":"Who held the office of pope during 1978?","choices":["Paul VI, John Paul I and John Paul II","Paul VI and John Paul I","John Paul I and John Paul II","John Paul II","Paul VI"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Paul VI died in August; John Paul I served for 33 days; John Paul II was elected in October.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"978b47899976371e2d6b80d38db48a88162564dd969852347887ddec8346c0c4"},{"qid":944,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q944","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"Events of 1948","stem":"Which of these events did NOT take place in 1948?","choices":["Launch of the Marshall Plan","Foundation of the People's Republic of China","Foundation of the State of Israel","Entry into force of the Italian Constitution","Split between Tito and the Soviet Union"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The People's Republic of China was proclaimed on 1 October 1949.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"093d462d5f378e16d899ace17cf565f5716b4842a7e0ad6c1cbe010808cee9bc"},{"qid":945,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q945","major":"Art History","era":"Historical","label":"Chronology of five sculptures","stem":"Put these works in chronological order: 1. Rodin's The Thinker; 2. Canova's monument to Maria Christina of Austria; 3. Fontana's Spatial Concept, Nature; 4. Riace Bronzes; 5. Michelangelo's Rondanini Pietà.","choices":["4; 5; 2; 1; 3","2; 5; 3; 1; 4","4; 3; 2; 5; 1","2; 4; 5; 1; 3","1; 2; 3; 4; 5"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The sequence runs from ancient Greek bronzes through Michelangelo, Canova and Rodin to twentieth-century Fontana.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"87214b09dee3a15d84f0d8c0432837d24bed9d167eaffd0ebd40ce20e7704392"},{"qid":946,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q946","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Sant'Apollinare in Classe","stem":"The church shown is Sant'Apollinare in Classe. Which city-based regional architectural tradition does it exemplify?","choices":["Early Christian","Ravennate (Ravenna)","Gothic","Renaissance","Ancient Roman"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Sant'Apollinare in Classe belongs to the Ravenna tradition. It is also Early Christian; specifying the regional tradition distinguishes the two overlapping original categories.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[625,518,970,762]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":345,"height":244},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"a5c0523a42d36c1dfd105b7dec036692932559e8e0f72ff9ddbb4f2522177a0c","visualSubject":"Sant'Apollinare in Classe","visualConcept":"Visual identification or ordering","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a verified public-domain/licensed reproduction or an original labelled schematic for generated variants.","originalRecord":{"qid":946,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q946","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Sant'Apollinare in Classe","stem":"To which artistic tradition does the church shown belong?","choices":["Early Christian","Ravennate (Ravenna)","Gothic","Renaissance","Ancient Roman"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The pictured church is Sant'Apollinare in Classe, near Ravenna. The source chooses the Ravenna tradition, but Early Christian is also applicable.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"Early Christian and Ravennate both apply to the pictured church.","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[625,518,970,762]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":345,"height":244},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"50e8877f17c4bee3bc08f3147374481dbc7297497466884eb292303b7bef15dd","visualSubject":"Sant'Apollinare in Classe","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use an original labelled schematic preserving geometry for generated variants."},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"B"},{"qid":947,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q947","major":"Architecture","era":"Contemporary","label":"Contemporary towers and architects","stem":"Match the towers to their architects: 1. Swiss Re, London; 2. Agbar, Barcelona; 3. Intesa Sanpaolo, Turin; 4. Freedom Tower, Manhattan; 5. New Regione Lombardia headquarters, Milan. Architects: a. Jean Nouvel; b. David Childs / SOM; c. Pei Cobb Freed & Partners; d. Norman Foster; e. Renzo Piano.","choices":["1b; 2a; 3c; 4d; 5e","1c; 2d; 3e; 4a; 5b","1b; 2d; 3c; 4a; 5e","1d; 2a; 3e; 4b; 5c","1d; 2e; 3a; 4b; 5c"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The completed towers are Foster's Swiss Re, Nouvel's Agbar, Piano's Intesa Sanpaolo, Childs/SOM's One World Trade Center and Pei Cobb Freed & Partners' Palazzo Lombardia. Libeskind led an earlier World Trade Center master plan, not the final tower design.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.som.com/projects/one-world-trade-center/"],"contentFingerprint":"896d2a6dd62397d5ca1a64f0697410e6540a56ad94a0a3a75876ee9c8aa839d1","originalRecord":{"qid":947,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q947","major":"Architecture","era":"Contemporary","label":"Contemporary towers and architects","stem":"Match the towers to their architects: 1. Swiss Re, London; 2. Agbar, Barcelona; 3. Intesa Sanpaolo, Turin; 4. Freedom Tower, Manhattan; 5. New Regione Lombardia headquarters, Milan. Architects: a. Jean Nouvel; b. Daniel Libeskind; c. Pei Cobb Freed & Partners; d. Norman Foster; e. Renzo Piano.","choices":["1b; 2a; 3c; 4d; 5e","1c; 2d; 3e; 4a; 5b","1b; 2d; 3c; 4a; 5e","1d; 2a; 3e; 4b; 5c","1d; 2e; 3a; 4b; 5c"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The printed key refers to Libeskind's early Freedom Tower proposal. The completed One World Trade Center was designed by David Childs of SOM.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3600.jpeg","sourcePage":101,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The printed attribution is for an early proposal, not the completed One World Trade Center.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"eaafc530be3b3a250ae644d210acb71650dd27ad39c4667df7eb70fd7bbdbf1b"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"D"},{"qid":948,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q948","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Liberty Leading the People","stem":"Who painted the work shown, dated 1830?","choices":["Claude Monet","Édouard Manet","Gustave Courbet","Eugène Delacroix","Théodore Géricault"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Delacroix painted Liberty Leading the People in response to the July Revolution of 1830.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3601.jpeg","sourcePage":102,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3601.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[248,224,594,501]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":346,"height":277},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"155f8c7e6cce6e8261c5c3a3a7650f0d6045aef77b1de37621ddfd91a6a48648","visualSubject":"Liberty Leading the People","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":949,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q949","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Teutoburg Forest and Augustus","stem":"Who was the Roman emperor at the time of the Battle of the Teutoburg Forest?","choices":["Trajan","Hadrian","Nero","Augustus","Caligula"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The defeat of Varus's legions occurred in AD 9, during Augustus's reign.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3601.jpeg","sourcePage":102,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"c