Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Etruscan origins: superseded claim","stem":"According to the studies cited by the source, from which region did the Etruscans originate, confirming Herodotus's account?","choices":["Asia Minor","Pontus","China","India","Lebanon"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The book selects Asia Minor. Later ancient-DNA research does not support a recent migration from Anatolia, so this item is withheld from scored practice.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The cited Etruscan-origin claim is superseded by ancient-DNA research.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"52a4ee74e872e5a231f2e0865b274904c46724d2d98e4f3eb80b21cb7186fd1c"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"A"},{"qid":958,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q958","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"Mantegna's Camera degli Sposi","stem":"Identify the work and artist of the fresco detail shown.","choices":["Camera degli Sposi — Andrea Mantegna","The Trinity — Masaccio","Diptych of the Dukes of Urbino — Piero della Francesca","Primavera — Sandro Botticelli","Sistine Chapel ceiling — Michelangelo"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The illusionistic oculus belongs to Mantegna's Camera degli Sposi in Mantua.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[132,360,440,665]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":308,"height":305},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"4366a61d42fd202aebbc958dd119f4d8c842bb72851973354f92e57cc74dc78c","visualSubject":"Mantegna's Camera degli Sposi","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":959,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q959","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Dada and found objects","stem":"Which description best matches Dada?","choices":["Dynamism, speed and confidence in technological progress","Disillusion, cynicism and an unemotional description of reality","Consumer objects and myths expressed through collage, photography and cinema","Chance, irony and found objects such as bicycles and bottle racks","Overcoming physical appearances to reveal a hidden second reality"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Dada used chance, irony and ready-made objects to challenge conventional definitions of art.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"20a7734c3f09b9c114281d7f168dc4d9107900bf878112dc6244c91c1bebbf6b"},{"qid":960,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q960","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Servius Tullius in Roman tradition","stem":"Which king of Rome was born a slave, according to tradition?","choices":["Romulus","Tarquinius Superbus","Ancus Marcius","Tarquinius Priscus","Servius Tullius"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Roman tradition associates Servius Tullius with servile origins; this is a legendary account rather than securely established biography.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"3b6b6f4ef65f8ff68812d31a7e87476fbd0884ec86229b33729867fdf6bc855f"},{"qid":961,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q961","major":"Architecture","era":"Contemporary","label":"Museum projects and architects","stem":"Match the museum projects to their architects: 1. Musée d'Orsay, Paris; 2. Guggenheim, Bilbao; 3. Tate Modern, London; 4. Jewish Museum, Berlin; 5. MART, Rovereto. Architects: a. Frank O. Gehry; b. Daniel Libeskind; c. Gae Aulenti; d. Mario Botta; e. Herzog & de Meuron.","choices":["5d; 2a; 4b; 3e; 1c","3e; 1c; 2d; 4b; 5a","4e; 2c; 3a; 1d; 5b","1c; 2a; 5b; 3e; 4d","2c; 1a; 4e; 3d; 5b"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Aulenti designed the Orsay museum conversion; Gehry designed Bilbao, Herzog & de Meuron Tate Modern, Libeskind Berlin and Botta MART.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"c589b1d0ff10996a030b0361cc1b3153aec84b5a59ff6611a3efcff247c84152"},{"qid":962,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q962","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"Suffragettes","stem":"Who were the suffragettes in England?","choices":["Women campaigning for morality","Women caring for wounded soldiers","Women engaged in charitable work","Women campaigning for the right to vote","Nurses"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Suffragettes campaigned for women's voting rights, notably through militant action in the early twentieth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"e97e36a691666545ef8e7592bfe2a7e08b19bc642c35bf72dd156fe0964427f0"},{"qid":963,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q963","major":"History & Politics","era":"Historical","label":"Declaration of Independence drafting committee","stem":"Who was NOT a member of the committee appointed to draft the American Declaration of Independence in 1776?","choices":["George Washington","Robert Livingston","Roger Sherman","John Adams","Thomas Jefferson"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Committee of Five consisted of Jefferson, Adams, Franklin, Sherman and Livingston.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"0cf559c47944032814d02b9bdc8ad78ac0600b1e7e0158bfdecd01675d061adb"},{"qid":964,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q964","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","label":"Villa Farnesina and Peruzzi","stem":"Which residence was NOT designed by Andrea Palladio?","choices":["Villa Farnesina","Villa Badoera","Villa Pojana","Villa Foscari, La Malcontenta","Villa Almerico Capra, La Rotonda"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Villa Farnesina in Rome was designed by Baldassare Peruzzi.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"456891bf330cc4c3cf9e23e3c494ec93965ed5864604355e5674ed8052d2ab4d"},{"qid":965,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q965","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Chronology of Roman battles","stem":"Arrange the battles chronologically: a. Cannae; b. Philippi; c. Pharsalus; d. Actium; e. Zama.","choices":["a; e; c; b; d","e; a; d; b; c","a; c; b; e; d","d; c; b; a; e","d; b; c; e; a"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Cannae occurred in 216 BC, Zama in 202, Pharsalus in 48, Philippi in 42 and Actium in 31.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3602.jpeg","sourcePage":103,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"6b78a95ad1a83067aadd71ce97249ab185126d827433dd6a32718fc453677206"},{"qid":966,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q966","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Contemporary","label":"Iraq's neighbours","stem":"Which country does NOT border Iraq?","choices":["Turkey","Iran","Kuwait","Israel","Jordan"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Iraq borders Turkey, Iran, Kuwait and Jordan, but not Israel.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"f7801ca654844b5e6dd26eeedf8b9e269bd4f9ce21c9c2ff87492c2a10280cb2"},{"qid":967,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q967","major":"Science & Tech","era":"Contemporary","label":"Gorillas","stem":"Which is the largest primate native to central Africa?","choices":["Orangutan","Chimpanzee","Gorilla","Gibbon","Baboon"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Gorillas are the largest living primates and are native to equatorial Africa.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"a854b605b78c4735a5fedbbb012d1e108d6d3453e9f10f922e75e64e847e8289"},{"qid":968,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q968","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Contemporary","label":"Italian spelling: coscienza","stem":"Which Italian word is misspelled?","choices":["Grigie","Superficie","Coscenza","Pesce","Guance"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The correct spelling is coscienza, with an i. This is an Italian-language vocabulary item.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"8fc428e30bb917034fd61fcabadc74ee306ce6d0874fd8ca18b5f4539e083351"},{"qid":969,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q969","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Contemporary","label":"Parliamentary control of the European Commission","stem":"Which institution exercises parliamentary control over the European Commission?","choices":["European Council","European Parliament","European Court","Each member state","No institution"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The European Parliament approves the Commission and can adopt a motion of censure against it.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"87c14e269daadeafb6fe2aaa75ff64f0f613d8bb77f5c7f748bb037815bd0a82"},{"qid":970,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q970","major":"Art History","era":"Historical","label":"Chronology of four painters","stem":"Which sequence places these painters in chronological order?","choices":["Mantegna; Delacroix; Miró; Renoir","Mantegna; Delacroix; Renoir; Miró","Renoir; Delacroix; Mantegna; Miró","Delacroix; Miró; Mantegna; Renoir","Mantegna; Renoir; Delacroix; Miró"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Their birth years are Mantegna 1431, Delacroix 1798, Renoir 1841 and Miró 1893.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"f4728997eb4400441af7a1fb846fa00a5a101f1e7d7085dd046899df5fe1c216"},{"qid":971,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q971","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Contemporary","label":"EU enlargement in 2007","stem":"Which two countries joined the European Union in 2007?","choices":["Bulgaria and Romania","Slovakia and the Czech Republic","Turkey and Albania","Montenegro and Malta","Luxembourg and Slovenia"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Bulgaria and Romania joined the EU on 1 January 2007.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"452225771e1ac1dcb5d241f51e8aba3b4e29f5a2a4f2615fcaaf13c0c66af6e9"},{"qid":972,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q972","major":"History & Politics","era":"Contemporary","label":"Laudato si'","stem":"What is the principal subject of Pope Francis's encyclical Laudato si'?","choices":["Faith","Faith and reason","Work and humanity","The social question","Ecology"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Laudato si', issued in 2015, addresses care for the environment and our common home.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"e1bce9a4b7112cc903c6133f51451910d911e71defbf8cfd83d6f72a390b8af0"},{"qid":973,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q973","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Art Informel and its chronology","stem":"Which description best defines Art Informel?","choices":["New York artists immediately after the war with different non-abstract, non-expressionist styles","Preference for exaggerated emotional data over objective reality","A Paris movement of the 1920s seeking to liberate the unconscious beyond reality","A post-war European tendency developing from the late 1940s, favouring spontaneous gesture and material rather than predetermined geometric composition","A Zurich movement of 1916–1920 rejecting established art through anti-art works"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Art Informel developed in post-war Europe from the late 1940s. It emphasized gesture, texture and spontaneous, non-geometric abstraction; the source's dating and definition are clarified.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.guggenheim-bilbao.eus/en/exhibitions/art-informel-and-abstract-expressionism-in-the-guggenheim-collections"],"contentFingerprint":"f9b6a06236a20c62885cb5e9a7084421c7ac75bf82c4499c58260cd38456f12a","originalRecord":{"qid":973,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q973","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Art Informel and its chronology","stem":"Which description defines Art Informel according to the source?","choices":["New York artists immediately after the war with different non-abstract, non-expressionist styles","Preference for exaggerated emotional data over objective reality","A Paris movement of the 1920s seeking to liberate the unconscious beyond reality","A European movement born in the 1950s–1960s rejecting recognizable figurative or abstract form","A Zurich movement of 1916–1920 rejecting established art through anti-art works"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The key selects the fourth description, but its dating is too late: Art Informel developed in post-war Europe from the late 1940s.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The stated origin decade of Art Informel is inaccurate.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"b0f17f9c25b5a56c58dcc859d25c46a6d2412966d285ea456b373c728d44b0a6"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"D"},{"qid":974,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q974","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Pollock and dripping","stem":"Which twentieth-century painter is associated with the dripping technique?","choices":["Mark Rothko","Piet Mondrian","Georges Braque","Roy Lichtenstein","Jackson Pollock"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Pollock poured and dripped paint onto canvases laid on the floor.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"682a7f7d0af9f95c5048b266add8b68dd2198637dbca1588a309c937d4afb44b"},{"qid":975,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q975","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"The Great Helmsman","stem":"Which twentieth-century leader was called the Great Helmsman?","choices":["Mao Tse-tung","Hirohito","Saddam Hussein","Idi Amin Dada","Ho Chi Minh"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The title Great Helmsman was used for Mao Zedong.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"b9de54df71013dd4441171cf446137f6c227016316d32cd6f2c4b62975554b39"},{"qid":976,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q976","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Siena Cathedral","stem":"Which architectural style characterizes the cathedral shown?","choices":["Romanesque","Gothic","Baroque","Renaissance","Byzantine"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Siena Cathedral is an important example of Italian Gothic architecture.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourcePage":104,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3603.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[744,1067,1104,1342]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":360,"height":275},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"55c2da7d1ec9bd564a67f4dd8be884eba51809b30601a5f554857f5a34d83bda","visualSubject":"Siena Cathedral","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use an original labelled schematic preserving geometry for generated variants."},{"qid":977,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q977","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Clerestory","stem":"What does clerestory refer to in a religious building?","choices":["A system of radial chapels","Two towers on the façade","A system of vaults","A band of windows illuminating the main nave","An element preceding the entrance"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A clerestory is the upper windowed part of a wall, admitting light above adjoining roofs.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"e198190e28d73f3229381ac6a13a13fb7172d3d78ddcc6dbd142733e268916a6"},{"qid":978,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q978","major":"History & Politics","era":"Historical","label":"Treaty of Campo Formio","stem":"Which treaty was signed by Napoleon and the Habsburgs on 17 October 1797?","choices":["Verdun","Versailles","Lunéville","Leoben","Campo Formio"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Treaty of Campo Formio concluded the War of the First Coalition.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"a8fa5aade94266d0b4f507f437b8d41f7165bdf234721f79b19aaf4e572f6fff"},{"qid":979,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q979","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Greek classical architecture and vaults","stem":"Which element was NOT commonly used in classical Greek architecture?","choices":["Column","Vault","Pediment","Moulding","Capital"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Classical Greek monumental architecture primarily used post-and-lintel construction; vaulting became especially characteristic of Roman architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"cb940398efc4c8324321cbc7761bfedbf928a9f64ba52d6ce2185c1aa5e36bba"},{"qid":980,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q980","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Byzantine impost blocks","stem":"In which architectural tradition is the pulvino especially characteristic?","choices":["Byzantine","Romanesque","Gothic","Baroque","Neoclassical"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The pulvino is an impost block between the capital and the springing of an arch, especially associated with Byzantine architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"9fa8d8ba01f0817f6810d2a3caee11506b6037c7e619ca1328c28a1404348bff"},{"qid":981,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q981","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Duchamp: Nude Descending a Staircase","stem":"Identify the artist and principal stylistic influences of the painting shown.","choices":["Futurism — Umberto Boccioni","Cubism — Pablo Picasso","Cubism — Georges Braque","Abstraction — Paul Klee","Cubist and Futurist influences — Marcel Duchamp"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Duchamp painted Nude Descending a Staircase (No. 2) in 1912, combining fractured Cubist forms with Futurist motion. It predates Dada's emergence in 1916.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[207,954,434,1328]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":227,"height":374},"verificationSources":["https://www.philamuseum.org/objects/51449"],"contentFingerprint":"28609f3ff6ce9b4ef9642927375bb79aa7b592d8910d3c036477bbd7e9d35e27","visualSubject":"Duchamp: Nude Descending a Staircase","visualConcept":"Visual identification or ordering","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a verified public-domain/licensed reproduction or an original labelled schematic for generated variants.","originalRecord":{"qid":981,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q981","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Duchamp: Nude Descending a Staircase","stem":"Identify the movement and artist of the painting shown, according to the source.","choices":["Futurism — Umberto Boccioni","Cubism — Pablo Picasso","Cubism — Georges Braque","Abstraction — Paul Klee","Dada — Marcel Duchamp"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The artist is Duchamp, but this 1912 painting predates Dada and combines Cubist and Futurist influences; none of the source's pairs is fully accurate.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"Duchamp is correct, but the pictured 1912 work predates Dada.","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[207,954,434,1328]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":227,"height":374},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"2c20df9b849bb5f00cb29f01e9fb269d3ecbe5aed12c5a61b6a0a19b351f41d9","visualSubject":"Duchamp: Nude Descending a Staircase","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"E"},{"qid":982,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q982","major":"Art History","era":"Historical","label":"Mezzo-rilievo","stem":"What does mezzo-rilievo mean?","choices":["A floor between the ground and first floors","A base raising a monumental public building above street level","A mixed painting and sculpture technique","A prehistoric sculpture","A relief intermediate between low relief and full relief"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Mezzo-rilievo means medium relief: the figures project more than in low relief, but less than in high relief.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"b684158926d0f46c373850a274fc4d843b19f8e68ab7e1ba2b1e3d0e565d17f6"},{"qid":983,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q983","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Definition of pulvino","stem":"What is a pulvino?","choices":["A support projecting from a wall","An inverted truncated-pyramid block between a capital and an arch's springing","A spiral ornament","An ornament of a Doric frieze","A concave semicircular moulding made of two arcs"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"A pulvino enlarges the bearing surface above a capital and supports the springing of the arch.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"3c4d221f727a3b1fb3f99fcfaf7dcf89f9aca2653e654cca16b5732b6e8da625"},{"qid":984,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q984","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Dalí's Mae West Room","stem":"Identify the artist and movement associated with the interior shown.","choices":["Antoni Gaudí — Art Nouveau","Salvador Dalí — Surrealism","Salvador Dalí — Metaphysical Art","Man Ray — Dada","Antoni Gaudí — Modernismo"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Dalí's Mae West Room arranges furniture and pictures to form a face when viewed from a particular position.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[675,748,883,1049]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":208,"height":301},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"78d3e5e995ee93588ff72cd682f9696f6f3b6a9b88feee03344d1f9c2e9e6cae","visualSubject":"Dalí's Mae West Room","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":985,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q985","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","label":"Principal architects of St Peter's","stem":"Which architect worked at St Peter's as an assistant to Maderno and Bernini, rather than being one of the principal architects named in the other options?","choices":["Francesco Borromini","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Donato Bramante","Michelangelo","Carlo Maderno"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Borromini worked at St Peter's under Maderno and Bernini, including work on the baldachin. The revised stem distinguishes assistance from the principal roles and no longer claims he made no contribution.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/francesco-borromini/"],"contentFingerprint":"c9e98ffc8f896b52ecec8d472f383b7978befd2b3474f99956c45f6795620d0e","originalRecord":{"qid":985,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q985","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","label":"Principal architects of St Peter's","stem":"Which architect did NOT contribute to the design of St Peter's Basilica in Rome, according to the source?","choices":["Francesco Borromini","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Donato Bramante","Michelangelo","Carlo Maderno"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The key excludes Borromini, but he worked at St Peter's as an assistant. The broad wording does not clearly distinguish principal design from collaboration.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3604.jpeg","sourcePage":105,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"Borromini worked at St Peter’s as an assistant; the wording is too broad.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"90da74a3628675bbdcc105fa689d92f550353aa661119f6ac5e3fcb3e34559f0"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"A"},{"qid":986,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q986","major":"Art History","era":"Historical","label":"Caravaggio: The Calling of Saint Matthew","stem":"In Caravaggio's painting shown, what are the men at the table doing when Christ calls Matthew?","choices":["Looking out of the window","Playing cards","Writing","Counting coins","Feasting"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The group is counting money, consistent with Matthew's occupation as a tax collector.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourcePage":106,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[293,258,627,586]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":334,"height":328},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"9d70dad48c8546fd8f85dc41a14f512b40a836bdaacd29df48dcc193dcca731a","visualSubject":"Caravaggio: The Calling of Saint Matthew","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":987,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q987","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","label":"Buildings and their cities","stem":"Which set correctly locates all four buildings?","choices":["Erechtheion: Delphi; Tempio Malatestiano: Urbino; Tempietto of San Pietro in Montorio: Rome; Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza: Rome","Erechtheion: Athens; Tempio Malatestiano: Urbino; Tempietto of San Pietro in Montorio: Rome; Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza: Rome","Erechtheion: Delphi; Tempio Malatestiano: Urbino; Tempietto of San Pietro in Montorio: Rome; Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza: Ivrea","Erechtheion: Athens; Tempio Malatestiano: Rimini; Tempietto of San Pietro in Montorio: Rome; Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza: Rome","Erechtheion: Athens; Tempio Malatestiano: Urbino; Tempietto of San Pietro in Montorio: Rome; Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza: Ivrea"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Erechtheion is in Athens, the Tempio Malatestiano in Rimini, and both the Tempietto and Sant'Ivo in Rome.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourcePage":106,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"908872e0c576dc894183c47c16e92c10d5837da5f8e16f68e2c8f7615e921fb3"},{"qid":988,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q988","major":"History & Politics","era":"Historical","label":"Chronology of five historical figures","stem":"Order these figures chronologically: a. John F. Kennedy; b. Frederick II; c. Pericles; d. Antonio Gramsci; e. Julius Caesar.","choices":["c; b; d; e; a","c; e; b; d; a","e; c; b; a; d","b; c; e; d; a","e; b; d; a; c"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Pericles precedes Caesar, followed by the medieval emperor Frederick II, Gramsci and Kennedy.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourcePage":106,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"366316a05141fffb2a2135ca005fb147a6fa3e139e1c3882243b958dc8336bab"},{"qid":989,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q989","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"Location of Leonardo's Last Supper","stem":"Where is Leonardo da Vinci's Last Supper located?","choices":["Santa Maria Novella, Florence","The refectory of Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan","Pisa Cathedral","The Louvre, Paris","The Vatican Museums, Rome"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Leonardo painted the mural on the refectory wall of Santa Maria delle Grazie in Milan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourcePage":106,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"f729518ee6ceb7b72461756152bdf53d31ac04fd5d587cc56deb43ed2b620beb"},{"qid":990,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q990","major":"Design","era":"Contemporary","label":"Five iconic Italian seats","stem":"Match the numbered seats in the illustration: a. Up — Gaetano Pesce; b. Sacco — Piero Gatti and Cesare Paolini; c. Sella — Achille and Pier Giacomo Castiglioni; d. Mezzadro — Achille and Pier Giacomo Castiglioni; e. Blow — Jonathan De Pas, Donato D'Urbino and Paolo Lomazzi.","choices":["1c; 2d; 3a; 4b; 5e","1d; 2c; 3e; 4b; 5a","1c; 2d; 3e; 4a; 5b","1c; 2e; 3a; 4d; 5b","1b; 2c; 3e; 4d; 5a"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The images show Sella, Mezzadro, Up, Sacco and Blow. Sacco's full design credits also include Franco Teodoro, omitted from the source's label.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourcePage":106,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[781,370,825,452],[757,469,832,545],[750,558,862,664],[754,679,842,786],[753,803,845,903]],"method":"numbered_source_crop_composite","essential":true,"width":900,"height":250},"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"4f68ec70ad9f07f658b599457c4d5384c8d07dbdf59fd40b5c46a52e4f63f92c","visualSubject":"Five iconic Italian seats","visualConcept":"Visual identification or numbered/lettered diagram mapping","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a public-domain or appropriately licensed reproduction of the same subject for generated variants; verify the licence and provenance before release."},{"qid":991,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q991","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Writing on the Ebla tablets","stem":"Which writing system appears on the ancient Ebla tablets found in Syria?","choices":["Cuneiform","Hieroglyphic","Boustrophedon","Cyrillic","Aramaic"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Ebla tablets use cuneiform. They are important early documents but not the earliest examples of writing, which predate them in Mesopotamia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourcePage":106,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":["https://www.britishmuseum.org/blog/how-write-cuneiform"],"contentFingerprint":"5adf09bcf2e6574e595ce258a819973347d9d90723e264d4729c9e83856553e8","originalRecord":{"qid":991,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q991","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Writing on the Ebla tablets","stem":"Which writing system appears on the Ebla tablets in Syria, described by the source as the first examples of writing?","choices":["Cuneiform","Hieroglyphic","Boustrophedon","Cyrillic","Aramaic"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Ebla tablets use cuneiform. They are not the world's earliest writing: earlier evidence comes from Mesopotamia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3605.jpeg","sourcePage":106,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"The Ebla tablets are not the earliest known writing.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"86aaad906c9f2b94d6eb50477b05d8b8f0d95c0e65e4437c31bc76616a8a76fb"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"A"},{"qid":992,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q992","major":"History & Politics","era":"Historical","label":"Chronology from Justinian to Charles V","stem":"Which sequence places the following figures in chronological order?","choices":["Charlemagne; Justinian; Charles V; Lorenzo the Magnificent; Frederick Barbarossa","Justinian; Charlemagne; Frederick Barbarossa; Lorenzo the Magnificent; Charles V","Justinian; Charles V; Lorenzo the Magnificent; Frederick Barbarossa; Charlemagne","Charlemagne; Charles V; Justinian; Frederick Barbarossa; Lorenzo the Magnificent","Frederick Barbarossa; Justinian; Charlemagne; Lorenzo the Magnificent; Charles V"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The sequence runs from the sixth-century emperor Justinian to Charlemagne, Barbarossa, Lorenzo de' Medici and Charles V.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"5e9370413631d982b1d7f045d8f921dcb674923dd73385a394de8c692dbb4999"},{"qid":993,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q993","major":"History & Politics","era":"Historical","label":"Marengo and the wars of 1848","stem":"Which battle does NOT belong with the others?","choices":["Pastrengo","Goito","Curtatone","Marengo","Montanara"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Marengo was fought in 1800; the other battles belong to the First Italian War of Independence in 1848.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"4eba9600c362e05448aa4d2179571b0f574df2a35e95e378f15932dbc527c44c"},{"qid":994,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q994","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Ancient","label":"Vindobona","stem":"Which present-day city was known in Latin as Vindobona?","choices":["Verona","Venice","Vienna","Vienne","Vercelli"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Vindobona was the Roman settlement on the site of modern Vienna.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"6760299a521a9356eb12ecf86e3b9fd3afe2c90fc620f5be7cf983a65e99dbb0"},{"qid":995,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q995","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Modern","label":"Hugo Pratt and Corto Maltese","stem":"Who created the comic-book character Corto Maltese?","choices":["Hugo Pratt","Tiziano Sclavi","Francesco Tullio Altan","Leo Ortolani","Benito Jacovitti"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Hugo Pratt introduced Corto Maltese in A Ballad of the Salty Sea in 1967.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"9b7887a04dfa15ef06f63a1b4b3b7487e83894844ce8d3483ce77609c43ef243"},{"qid":996,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q996","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Monreale: architecture and mosaics","stem":"Which style best describes the main architectural tradition of Monreale Cathedral, rather than its mosaic decoration?","choices":["Gothic","Byzantine","Baroque","Romanesque","Early Christian"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Monreale is a Norman Romanesque cathedral with Byzantine mosaics. Specifying the architecture resolves the original overlap between the two styles.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"99afd18e861f1622519a5bd07f2ba427a89892caac7d68a3934069b4198964d3","originalRecord":{"qid":996,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q996","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Monreale: architecture and mosaics","stem":"To which artistic tradition does Monreale Cathedral belong, according to the source?","choices":["Gothic","Byzantine","Baroque","Romanesque","Early Christian"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The cathedral's architecture is Norman Romanesque, while its mosaics are Byzantine. The source's broad wording makes two alternatives plausible.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"held","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"held","holdReason":"Romanesque architecture and Byzantine mosaics make the broad art classification ambiguous.","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"c3c99e88bee9b89f97afedc610d49652a23cb28426af7c84c40dadd459418771"},"correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","scoringKey":"D"},{"qid":997,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q997","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","label":"Magi as priests","stem":"Who were the Magi in the ancient world?","choices":["Officers","Kings","Priests","Civil servants","Merchants"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Magi originally referred to a priestly group associated with ancient Iranian religious traditions.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"f3debb1bbbf8b2702bea56f017ff6a93b324da67330032bcdaac1174548c8578"},{"qid":998,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q998","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"The Twentieth Congress of the CPSU","stem":"What did the Twentieth Congress of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union mark?","choices":["The arrest of Beria","Rejection of Marxism-Leninism","The invasion of Hungary","Dissolution of the Soviet empire","The end of Stalinism"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Khrushchev's 1956 denunciation of Stalin marked the beginning of de-Stalinization, rather than an immediate disappearance of all Stalinist practices.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"29633925668d30a4365ef0cb599c5372d2c52c88a77c2c2691236b7a7f498c76"},{"qid":999,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q999","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Transept","stem":"What is a transept?","choices":["A half-column projecting from a wall as decoration","The part of a church behind the altar and visible on the exterior","An inverted truncated-pyramid block between capital and arch","A transverse nave between the main nave and the apse","A small Romanesque entrance porch supported by columns"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The transept crosses the longitudinal nave, often giving a church its cross-shaped plan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"ad880f66d7be311e126962fa63713bce7be261df44be6530aa3df83cd616f122"},{"qid":1000,"id":"ALPHA-B011-Q1000","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","label":"Bouleuterion","stem":"What was a bouleuterion in an ancient Greek city?","choices":["The building where the city council met","The city's main square","The highest part of the city","The agricultural area outside the walls","The open-air part of a house"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The bouleuterion housed meetings of the boule, the city council.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","answerBasis":"Printed answer key IMG_3607.jpeg, checked against the photographed stem and choices.","language":"en","version":"source-en-v39","sourceImage":"IMG_3606.jpeg","sourcePage":107,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","visualMetadata":null,"verificationSources":[],"contentFingerprint":"521c515010beb6700e042b065d9f346b19c6b11cac8795ec0b6f1ee271e9059d"}]